he \\(n\\) holes.  \n   This uses exactly \\(n=2025\\) tiles, meeting the lower bound.  \n\nSince the lower and upper bounds coincide, the minimum number of tiles is \\(2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares.  \nMatilda places axis‑aligned rectangular tiles whose interiors are unions of whole unit squares; tiles may not overlap and each tile’s sides lie on grid lines.  \n\nDenote by \\(H\\) the set of uncovered unit squares.  \nThe condition “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square’’ implies  \n\n\\[\n|H|=n,\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad H=\\{(r,\\pi(r))\\mid r=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Tiles as row–column products  \n\nA tile is a rectangle, i.e. a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the tile consists of all cells \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r\\in R,\\;c\\in C\\).  \n\nBecause a tile may not contain any uncovered square, for each row \\(r\\in R\\) the column \\(\\pi(r)\\) (the column of the hole in that row) must be **absent** from \\(C\\).  \nConsequently, if a tile covers a set of rows \\(R\\) with \\(|R|=m\\), then the set of columns it may use is  \n\n\\[\nC = \\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\pi(r)\\mid r\\in R\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has cardinality \\(n-m\\).  \nHence the tile covers exactly  \n\n\\[\nm\\,(n-m) \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nunit squares.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Partition of the rows  \n\nSince tiles are disjoint, each row belongs to **exactly one** tile.  \nThus the rows are partitioned into groups  \n\n\\[\nR_1,R_2,\\dots ,R_k,\\qquad |R_i|=m_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) the total number of covered squares is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i\\,(n-m_i). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll squares except the \\(n\\) holes must be covered, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i\\,(n-m_i)=n^2-n. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we rewrite (4):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i\\,(n-m_i)\n&= n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i-\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i^{2} \\\\\n&= n^2-\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) therefore yields  \n\n\\[\nn^2-\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i^{2}=n^2-n\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i^{2}=n. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nFor any positive integer \\(m\\) we have \\(m^{2}\\ge m\\), with equality only when \\(m=1\\).  \nApplying this to each \\(m_i\\) in (5) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i^{2}\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = n,\n\\]\n\nand equality holds **iff** every \\(m_i=1\\).  \nSince (5) demands equality, we must have  \n\n\\[\nm_1=m_2=\\dots =m_k=1.\n\\]\n\nHence the number of groups (tiles) is  \n\n\\[\nk = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = n = 2025.\n\\]\n\nThus **at least \\(2025\\) tiles are necessary**.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nTake the permutation \\(\\pi\\) that determines the holes.  \nFor each row \\(r\\) ( \\(1\\le r\\le n\\) ) place a tile consisting of that single row together with **all** columns except the hole column \\(\\pi(r)\\).  \nFormally, the \\(r\\)-th tile is  \n\n\\[\nT_r = \\{r\\}\\times\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\pi(r)\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(T_r\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle, the tiles are pairwise disjoint (they occupy different rows), and together they cover every unit square that is not a hole.  \nExactly \\(n=2025\\) tiles are used.\n\nThus **\\(2025\\) tiles are sufficient**.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (no fewer than \\(2025\\) tiles) and the explicit construction (exactly \\(2025\\) tiles) coincide.  \nTherefore the minimal number of rectangular tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
