"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimal number of rectangular tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Model the uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to form a *permutation matrix*: there is a bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \n   Here \\(n=2025\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound (column argument).**  \n   For a fixed column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j\\) be the row of its uncovered square, i.e. \\(\\pi(r_j)=j\\).  \n   - If \\(r_j=1\\) (the uncovered square is at the top of the column) the remaining \\(n-1\\) squares of column \\(j\\) can be covered by a single vertical tile (rows \\(2,\\dots ,n\\), column \\(j\\)).  \n   - If \\(r_j=n\\) (the uncovered square is at the bottom) the remaining squares can be covered by a single vertical tile (rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\), column \\(j\\)).  \n   - If \\(2\\le r_j\\le n-1\\) the column is split by the uncovered square into a non\u2011empty part **above** and a non\u2011empty part **below**. Any tile covering column \\(j\\) must have a contiguous set of rows; it cannot contain both parts because that would also contain the uncovered square. Hence at least **two** tiles are needed for such a column.  \n\n   Since the rows \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) each contain exactly one uncovered square, there are exactly two columns (the columns of those two squares) with \\(r_j=1\\) or \\(r_j=n\\). All other \\(n-2\\) columns have \\(2\\le r_j\\le n-1\\). Consequently any tiling requires at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot (n-2)+2 = 2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n3. **Upper bound (explicit construction).**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a **vertical** tile covering rows \\(j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\) and column \\(j\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) place a **vertical** tile covering rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,j-1\\) and column \\(j\\).  \n\n   Each tile is a rectangle (contiguous rows, a single column).  \n   The two tiles belonging to the same column cover disjoint row intervals, so no two tiles overlap.  \n   The diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) lies in column \\(i\\) but is omitted from both the \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 and \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 tiles for that column, hence it remains uncovered.  \n   Every other cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs either to the \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 tile of column \\(j\\) (if \\(i<j\\)) or to the \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 tile of column \\(j\\) (if \\(i>j\\)). Thus all non\u2011diagonal squares are covered exactly once.  \n\n   The construction uses \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nWe have an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  \nA **tile** is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile therefore consists of a set  \n\n\\[\nR\\times C=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are *contiguous* intervals of rows and columns, respectively.  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint (no unit square is covered by more than one tile).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq\\pi(i)\\},\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by the tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   A column\u2011wise lower bound  \n\nFix a column \\(j\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nr_j:=\\pi^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the unique row whose uncovered square lies in column \\(j\\); thus \\((r_j,j)\\in H\\).\n\n*If \\(r_j=1\\).*  \nThe squares of column \\(j\\) that must be covered are \\((2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) squares lie in the contiguous row interval \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently a single vertical tile  \n\n\\[\n\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole column \\(j\\) without touching the uncovered square.\n\n*If \\(r_j=n\\).*  \nAnalogously, the single tile  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\times\\{j\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all required squares of column \\(j\\).\n\n*If \\(2\\le r_j\\le n-1\\).*  \nThe column is split by the uncovered square into two non\u2011empty parts:\n\n\\[\nA_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\},\\qquad\nB_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny tile that contains column \\(j\\) must have a row interval that is **contiguous**.  \nBecause the uncovered square \\((r_j,j)\\) is forbidden, a tile cannot contain rows both below and above \\(r_j\\); otherwise its row interval would contain \\(r_j\\) and the tile would contain the uncovered square.  \nHence a tile covering column \\(j\\) can cover **only** the set \\(A_j\\) *or* only the set \\(B_j\\).  \nSince both \\(A_j\\) and \\(B_j\\) are non\u2011empty, at least two distinct tiles are necessary to cover column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Counting the necessary tiles  \n\nRows \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) each contain exactly one uncovered square; let  \n\n\\[\nc_1:=\\pi(1),\\qquad c_n:=\\pi(n)\n\\]\n\nbe the columns of those two squares.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nr_{c_1}=1,\\qquad r_{c_n}=n .\n\\]\n\nThus the columns \\(c_1\\) and \\(c_n\\) belong to the first two cases above and need **one** tile each.  \nAll remaining \\(n-2\\) columns satisfy \\(2\\le r_j\\le n-1\\) and therefore require **at least two** tiles each.\n\nHence any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n1+1+2\\,(n-2)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the identity).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the **lower** vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{low}}:=\\{\\,j+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the **upper** vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{up}}:=\\{\\,1,\\dots ,j-1\\,\\}\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Properties of the construction.*\n\n*   **Rectangularity.**  \n    Each \\(T_j^{\\text{low}}\\) and \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\) is a product of a contiguous row interval and a single column; hence it is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.\n\n*   **Disjointness.**  \n    For a fixed column \\(j\\) the two tiles use the disjoint row intervals \\(\\{1,\\dots ,j-1\\}\\) and \\(\\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore they do not intersect.  \n    Tiles belonging to different columns occupy different columns, so they are also disjoint.\n\n*   **Uncovered squares.**  \n    The diagonal cell \\((i) lies in column \\(i\\).  \n    The lower tile of column \\(i\\) covers rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) and the upper tile covers rows \\(1,\\dots ,i-1\\); neither contains row \\(i\\).  \n    Hence \\((i,i)\\) remains uncovered.\n\n*   **Coverage of all other squares.**  \n    Let \\((i,j)\\) be a non\u2011diagonal cell.  \n    If \\(i<j\\) then \\(i\\) belongs to the row interval \\(\\{1,\\dots ,j-1\\}\\) of the upper tile \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\); thus \\((i,j)\\) is covered by that tile.  \n    If \\(i>j\\) then \\(i\\) belongs to the row interval \\(\\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of the lower tile \\(T_j^{\\text{low}}\\); thus \\((i,j)\\) is covered by that tile.  \n    Consequently every square of \\(U\\) is covered exactly once.\n\n*   **Number of tiles.**  \n    There are \\(n-1\\) lower tiles (for \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) and \\(n-1\\) upper tiles (for \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\)), totalling  \n\n    \\[\n    (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n    \\]\n\n    tiles.\n\nThus the construction meets the lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Conclusion  \n\nEvery admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient. \u220e"
