ct tiles are needed. This is correct.\n\n- \"Rows 1 and n each contain exactly one uncovered square; let c_1 = π(1), c_n = π(n) be the columns of those two squares. Consequently r_{c_1} = 1, r_{c_n} = n.\" This is correct.\n\n- \"Thus the columns c_1 and c_n belong to the first two cases above and need one tile each. All remaining n-2 columns satisfy 2 ≤ r_j ≤ n-1 and therefore require at least two tiles each.\" This is correct.\n\n- \"Hence any admissible tiling uses at least 1+1+2*(n-2) = 2n-2 tiles.\" correct.\n\n- Construction: identity permutation, uncovered squares on diagonal. For each column j with 1 ≤ j ≤ n-1 place lower vertical tile covering rows j+1..n. For each column j with 2 ≤ j ≤ n place upper vertical tile covering rows 1..j-1. They claim disjointness: For a fixed column j, the two tiles use disjoint row intervals {1,...,j-1} and {j+1,...,n}. Yes, they are disjoint; they do not intersect at row j. Tiles belonging to different columns occupy different columns, so disjoint. Good.\n\n- Uncovered squares: diagonal cell (i,i) lies in column i. The lower tile of column i covers rows i+1..n, upper tile covers rows 1..i-1; neither contains row i. So (i,i) remains uncovered. Good.\n\n- Coverage of all other squares: For non-diagonal cell (i,j), if i<j then i belongs to row interval {1,...,j-1} of upper tile T_j^up, so covered. If i>j then i belongs to row interval {j+1,...,n} of lower tile T_j^low, so covered. So all non-diagonal squares are covered exactly once. Good.\n\n- Number of tiles: n-1 lower tiles + n-1 upper tiles = 2n-2. Good.\n\nThus the solution appears correct, reasoning valid, no critical errors.\n\nPotential minor issues: The solution uses \"the diagonal cell (i) lies in column i.\" That seems like a typo; they probably meant \"(i,i)\". But this is a minor typographical error, not a critical error. Also they say \"The diagonal cell (i) lies in column i.\" It's missing a coordinate, but it's clear from context. This is a justification gap (typ