gle\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle covering the cells to the right of the diagonal in row \\(i\\).\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) rectangle covering the cells below the diagonal in column \\(j\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{C_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint:\n- A cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to exactly one rectangle, namely \\(R_i\\).\n- A cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) belongs to exactly one rectangle, namely \\(C_j\\).\n- No rectangle contains a diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).\n\nThus the \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles cover every non‑diagonal unit square exactly once and avoid all diagonal squares.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{there exists a tiling with }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nCombining the lower bound (1) with the explicit construction (2) shows that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Explicit example (the “one = 1’’ case)\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal:\n\\[\n\\text{uncovered cells}= \\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le2025\\}.\n\\]\n\nTiles:\n- For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le2024\\) place a tile of size \\(1\\times(2025-i)\\) covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) for \\(j=i+1,\\dots ,2025\\).\n- For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le2025\\) place a tile of size \\((2025-j)\\times1\\) covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) for \\(i=j+1,\\dots ,2025\\).\n\nThese \\(2024+2024=4048\\) rectangles are disjoint, avoid the diagonal cells, and together cover every other unit square.  Hence they constitute an explicit optimal tiling.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
