(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j:=p^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the row of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Distinguished cells\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(r_j-1,j)\\mid r_j>1\\},\\qquad   \nB:=\\{(r_j+1,j)\\mid r_j<n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T\\) consists of the *topmost* covered cells of the columns, \\(B\\) of the *bottommost* covered cells.\n\nBecause exactly one column has its uncovered square in the top row (\\(r_j=1\\)), all other \\(n-1\\) columns satisfy \\(r_j>1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|T|=n-1.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, exactly one column has its uncovered square in the bottom row (\\(r_j=n\\)), so  \n\n\\[\n|B|=n-1.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|T\\cup B|=|T|+|B|=2(n-1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma: a rectangle contains at most one distinguished cell\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\\text{No axis‑parallel rectangle can contain two distinct squares from }T\\cup B.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(R\\) be a rectangle covering two distinct cells \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) from \\(T\\cup B\\).\n\n*Case 1:* Both \\(x,y\\in T\\).  \nWrite \\(x=(r_{j_1}-1,j_1)\\) and \\(y=(r_{j_2}-1,j_2)\\) with \\(j_1\\neq j_2\\).  \nSince the rows \\(r_{j_1}-1\\) and \\(r_{j_2}-1\\) are distinct (the numbers \\(r_j\\) are a permutation), the rectangle’s row interval must contain both of them, hence also the row \\(r_{j_1}\\) (which lies strictly between them).  \nBut the cell \\((r_{j_1},j_1)\\) is the uncovered square of column \\(j_1\\); therefore \\(R\\) would contain a hole – impossible.  \n\n*Case 2:* Both \\(x,y\\in B\\).  \nWrite \\(x=(r_{j_1}+1,j_1)\\), \\(y=(r_{j_2}+1,j_2)\\).  \nAgain the rows \\(r_{j_1}+1\\) and \\(r_{j_2}+1\\) are distinct, so the rectangle’s row interval contains the row \\(r_{j_1}\\), which is the uncovered square of column \\(j_1\\). Contradiction.\n\n*Case 3:* One cell in \\(T\\), the other in \\(B\\).  \nAssume \\(x\\in T\\) and \\(y\\in B\\).  \nIf the row of \\(x\\) is above the row of \\(y\\) (i.e. \\(r_{j_1}-1<r_{j_2}+1\\)), then the rectangle’s row interval contains the row \\(r_{j_1}\\), the hole of column \\(j_1\\); otherwise it contains the row \\(r_{j_2}\\), the hole of column \\(j_2\\). In either sub‑case a hole lies inside \\(R\\), contradiction.\n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction; hence the lemma holds. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEvery tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle, and by the lemma each tile can cover **at most one** cell of the set \\(T\\cup B\\).  \nSince \\(|T\\cup B|=2(n-1)\\) by (1), any tiling of the board’s complement must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define two (possibly empty) rectangles:\n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{up}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\\qquad\nR_j^{\\text{down}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n- If \\(j=1\\), \\(R_1^{\\text{up}}\\) is empty; if \\(j=n\\), \\(R_n^{\\text{down}}\\) is empty.  \n- For \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) the rectangle \\(R_j^{\\text{up}}\\) is a vertical tile of height \\(j-1\\).  \n- For \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) the rectangle \\(R_j^{\\text{down}}\\) is a vertical tile of height \\(n-j\\).\n\n**Verification (point‑by‑point).**  \nFix a column \\(j\\).  \nThe two rectangles \\(R_j^{\\text{up}}\\) and \\(R_j^{\\text{down}}\\) together cover all rows of that column except row \\(j\\); row \\(j\\) is precisely the uncovered diagonal square \\((j,j)\\).  \nSince different columns are disjoint, the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\{R_j^{\\text{up}}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\cup\\{R_j^{\\text{down}}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers **every** non‑diagonal