\nIf the row of \\(x\\) is above the row of \\(y\\) (i.e. \\(r_{j_1}-1<r_{j_2}+1\\)), then the rectangle’s row interval contains the row \\(r_{j_1}\\), the hole of column \\(j_1\\); otherwise it contains the row \\(r_{j_2}\\), the hole of column \\(j_2\\). In either sub‑case a hole lies inside \\(R\\), contradiction.\n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction; hence the lemma holds. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEvery tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle, and by the lemma each tile can cover **at most one** cell of the set \\(T\\cup B\\).  \nSince \\(|T\\cup B|=2(n-1)\\) by (1), any tiling of the board’s complement must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define two (possibly empty) rectangles:\n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{up}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\\qquad\nR_j^{\\text{down}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n- If \\(j=1\\), \\(R_1^{\\text{up}}\\) is empty; if \\(j=n\\), \\(R_n^{\\text{down}}\\) is empty.  \n- For \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) the rectangle \\(R_j^{\\text{up}}\\) is a vertical tile of height \\(j-1\\).  \n- For \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) the rectangle \\(R_j^{\\text{down}}\\) is a vertical tile of height \\(n-j\\).\n\n**Verification (point‑by‑point).**  \nFix a column \\(j\\).  \nThe two rectangles \\(R_j^{\\text{up}}\\) and \\(R_j^{\\text{down}}\\) together cover all rows of that column except row \\(j\\); row \\(j\\) is precisely the uncovered diagonal square \\((j,j)\\).  \nSince different columns are disjoint, the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\{R_j^{\\text{up}}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\cup\\{R_j^{\\text{down}}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers **every** non‑diagonal unit square exactly once and covers **no** diagonal square.  \nAll rectangles have sides on grid lines, so they are valid tiles.\n\nThe number of non‑empty rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nEquation (2) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while (3) shows that this bound is attainable.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n* All mathematical statements are enclosed in TeX delimiters.  \n* The lower‑bound proof correctly uses the distinguished cells \\(T\\) and \\(B\\) and the lemma that no rectangle can contain two of them.  \n* The construction is explicitly checked column‑by‑column, showing that each non‑diagonal square is covered exactly once and no diagonal square is covered.  \n* No extraneous commentary or alternative approaches appear in the “Detailed Solution”.  \n* The final answer is presented in \\boxed{} format."
