umn has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \n5. **Conclusion:** The lower bound and the construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle whose interior consists of unit squares; tiles may not overlap and may not contain any uncovered square.  \nConsequently every tile lies completely either\n\n* to the **left** of the hole in each of its rows, i.e. in the set  \n  \\[\n  L=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n  \\]\n  or\n\n* to the **right** of the hole in each of its rows, i.e. in the set  \n  \\[\n  R=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\nThus a tiling of the board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).  \nWe treat the two regions separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left region \\(L\\)\n\nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is exactly one row \\(i_c\\) with \\(p(i_c)=c+1\\) (because \\(p\\) is a permutation).  \nHence the cell \\((i_c,c)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and is the **rightmost** cell of row \\(i_c\\) that lies in \\(L\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) that contains \\((i_c,c)\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\) must avoid the hole \\((i_c,c+1)\\), its column interval cannot extend beyond column \\(c\\); therefore the column interval of \\(Q\\) is precisely \\([1,c]\\).  \nConsequently a rectangle covering \\((i_c,c)\\) is uniquely determined by its row interval and must have column interval \\([1,c]\\).\n\nIf two distinct columns \\(c\\neq c'\\) were covered by the same rectangle, that rectangle would have to contain both column intervals \\([1,c]\\) and \\([1,c']\\), i.e. it would contain column \\(\\max\\{c,c'\\}\\).  \nBut for the larger of the two columns, say \\(c'>c\\), the cell \\((i_{c'},c')\\) would lie in the rectangle while its hole \\((i_{c'},c'+1)\\) would also belong to the rectangle (because the column interval includes \\(c'+1\\)), contradicting the definition of a tile.  \n\nHence **different columns \\(c\\) require different rectangles**.  \nSince there are \\(n-1\\) columns \\(c\\) (namely \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\)), any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right region \\(R\\)\n\nThe right region is symmetric to the left one: after reflecting the board horizontally, the uncovered squares become a permutation and the right region becomes a left region.  \nApplying the argument of §1 to the reflected board yields that any tiling of \\(R\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nSince a tile cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\), the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling is at least  \n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal:\n\\[\n\\text{uncovered squares }=\\{(i,i):1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\\text{so }p(i)=i\\ \\forall i .\n\\]\n\n*For the left side.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a vertical tile\n\\[\nV_i=\\{(i+1,i),(i+2,i),\\dots ,(n,i)\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\((n-i)\\times1\\).  \nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover exactly the cells of \\(L\\).\n\n*For the right side.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a horizontal tile\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\},\n\\]\na rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).  \nThese tiles are also pairwise disjoint and together cover exactly the cells of \\(R\\).\n\nThe two families \\(\\{V_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) are disjoint from each other because a vertical tile lies entirely in column \\(i\\) while a horizontal tile lies entirely in row \\(i\\); the only common cell would be \\((i,i)\\), which is uncovered.  \n\nThus we have constructed a tiling consisting of\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles, and each row and each column contains precisely the diagonal uncovered square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nThe construction uses exactly the number of tiles given by the lower bound (1).  \nTherefore the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Numerical answer\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\):\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the required minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
