ecause Q contains (i_{c'}, c') and (i_c, c), the row interval includes both i_{c'} and i_c. So yes, i_{c'} is in row interval. So uncovered square (i_{c'},c'+1) is in Q. So contradiction. So Lemma holds.\n\nThus each rectangle can contain at most one distinguished cell, giving at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover L. Similarly for R.\n\nThus reasoning is valid.\n\nNow check presentation: Are there any gaps? The solution is fairly thorough. However, there might be some minor gaps: The proof that each tile lies entirely in L or R uses \"Since J is an interval, for each i either J ⊆ [1,p(i)-1] or J ⊆ [p(i)+1,n]\". This is correct because p(i) not in J, and J is contiguous interval, so either all columns are left of p(i) or all are right. That's fine.\n\nThe proof that all p(i) for i in I must be all ≤ min J - 1 or all ≥ max J + 1 uses \"If there exist i,i' with p(i) < p(i'), then the two possibilities cannot both hold: J cannot be simultaneously a subset of [1,p(i)-1] and of [p(i')+1,n] unless J empty.\" This is correct.\n\nPotential gap: The solution says \"Thus a tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of L and a tiling of R.\" That's fine.\n\nIn Lemma 1, they assume that the rectangle Q contains row i_{c'} and column c'+1, but they need to argue that the row interval includes i_{c'} (true) and column interval includes c'+1 (true). So uncovered cell is inside Q. That's fine.\n\nPotential gap: The solution uses reflection argument for R, but does not explicitly prove that the reflection preserves the property that each tile lies entirely in left region of reflected board. But it's symmetric, so acceptable.\n\nPotential gap: The construction uses vertical tiles V_i and horizontal tiles H_i. They claim these are rectangles (true). They claim they are disjoint (they prove intersection can't happen). They claim coverage of all cells except diagonal (they argue for any cell (r,c) with r<c belongs to H_r, r>c belongs to V_c). That's correct. So no gaps