\).  \nA \\(2025\\times2025\\) board consists of unit squares whose coordinates we denote by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\); the first coordinate indicates the row, the second the column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Normalisation of the uncovered squares  \n\nThe requirement “each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square’’ means that the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nPermuting the columns of the board (i.e. relabelling column \\(j\\) as column \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\)) does not affect the possibility of placing tiles nor the number of tiles needed. After this relabelling the uncovered squares become the main diagonal:\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence from now on we assume that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Matrix description of the covering  \n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix \\(A=(a_{ij})\\) by\n\\[\na_{ij}=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, &\\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered},\\\\[2pt]\n0, &\\text{if }(i,j)\\in U\\text{ (uncovered)} .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause the only uncovered squares are the diagonal ones, we have\n\\[\nA=J-I,\n\\]\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. A tile as a rank‑1 matrix  \n\nConsider a rectangular tile \\(T\\). Let \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) be the set of rows occupied by \\(T\\) and \\(C\\subseteq[n]\\) the set of columns it occupies. Since a tile may not cover an uncovered square, we must have\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(u_R\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) be the characteristic vector of \\(R\\) (i.e. \\((u_R)