                            "text": "We need to verify the solution given for the problem: \"minimum number of tiles needed such that each row and each column has exactly one unit square not covered by any tile.\" The solution claims answer is 2025. Let's examine.\n\nWe need to apply hierarchical decision tree: Level 1: answer correctness. Level 2: reasoning validity (methods). Level 3: presentation issues.\n\nWe must not re-prove but evaluate the solution. The solution uses rank argument: each tile is a rank-1 matrix, sum of rank-1 matrices equals J - I, which has rank n, so at least n tiles. Then they construct n tiles: each row i tile covering all squares in row i except diagonal (i,i). That's a 1 x (n-1) rectangle covering all squares in row i except the uncovered diagonal. Does that satisfy condition that each column also has exactly one uncovered square? Yes, each column j has uncovered square at (j,j). The tiles are disjoint because each tile occupies a distinct row. So each column j is covered by all tiles except tile j (which covers all squares in column j except (j,j)? Wait, tile i covers row i, all columns j != i. So column j is covered by all tiles i != j. So column j has uncovered (j,j). So each column has exactly one uncovered square. So the construction works. So answer 2025 is correct.\n\nNow check reasoning: They used matrix representation, rank argument, outer product representation of tile as rank-1 matrix. This is a valid method: linear algebra rank bound. The reasoning is mathematically sound. The tile representation as outer product is correct because tile occupies a set of rows and columns, with no overlap of rows and columns (R_T ∩ C_T = ∅). Actually, a rectangular tile occupies contiguous rows and columns? The problem says rectangular tiles possibly of different sizes, each side on grid lines. So a tile is a rectangle of some rows and columns. The representation as product of characteristic vectors of rows and columns is correct: tile covers