 each column. Hence there exists a permutation σ such that uncovered squares are (i,σ(i)).\" That's correct.\n\nThen they permute columns to assume diagonal uncovered squares. That's fine.\n\nPotential missing justification: They claim \"the sum of the indicator matrices equals A\". Since each unit square is covered by at most one tile and uncovered squares are zero, the sum of indicator matrices equals A. That's correct.\n\nPotential gap: They claim \"The rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks\". That's a known inequality: rank(A+B) ≤ rank(A) + rank(B). So valid.\n\nPotential gap: They compute eigenvalues of J - I: eigenvalues n-1 (once) and -1 (multiplicity n-1). That's correct. So rank = n.\n\nPotential gap: The construction uses tiles T_i = {(i,j) | j ≠ i}. That's a rectangle? Actually T_i is row i, columns all except column i. That's not a contiguous rectangle: it's a row with a missing column i, which is a row of length n-1 but with a hole at column i. However, the tile must be a rectangle, i.e., contiguous block of unit squares. The set {(i,j) | j ≠ i} is not a rectangle because it has a missing square at (i,i). But the tile cannot include (i,i) because it's uncovered. However, a rectangle covering row i except column i would be two separate rectangles: one from columns 1 to i-1, and another from i+1 to n. The solution claims T_i is a 1 × (n-1) rectangle covering all squares of row i except the diagonal square. But a 1 × (n-1) rectangle cannot have a hole; it must be contiguous. So T_i as defined is not a rectangle because it excludes a single column in the middle (if i is not at an endpoint). For i not at 1 or n, the shape is not a rectangle. For i=1, T_1 = {(1,j) | j ≠ 1} = columns 2..n, which is contiguous (1 × (n-1) rectangle). For i=n, T_n = {(n,j) | j ≠ n} = columns 1..n-1, also contiguous. But for interior rows, the shape is two disjoint intervals, not a rectangle.\n\nThus the construction may be invalid: each tile must be a rectangle, i.e., contiguous rows and columns. The solution's construction uses a shape that is not a rectangle for interior rows. So the construction is flawed. However, perhaps they consider a rectangle that can have a missing column? No, definition: rectangular tile of unit squares with sides on grid lines. That means a rectangle is a contiguous block of unit squares. So the shape must be a product of intervals: rows from a to b, columns from c to d. The set {(i,j) | j ≠ i} is not a product of intervals because the column set is not an interval (it is [1,n] \\ {i}). So it's not a rectangle. Therefore the construction fails to satisfy the tile shape requirement. So the solution's construction is invalid.\n\nBut maybe they could adjust: they could use two rectangles per row (one left of diagonal, one right of diagonal). That would double the number of tiles, giving 2n - something. But they need exactly n tiles. So perhaps there is a different construction that uses n rectangles? Let's think: Could we tile the grid with n rectangles such that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square? The diagonal uncovered squares are given. Each tile must be a rectangle that does not include any diagonal squares. The uncovered squares are on the diagonal. So each rectangle must avoid diagonal squares. Could we have n rectangles each covering a whole row except diagonal? That would be non-rectangular as argued. So maybe we can use vertical rectangles covering columns except diagonal, also non-rectangular. So perhaps the minimal number of rectangles is larger than n? But the solution claims n is minimal. Let's test with small n to see if it's possible with n rectangles. For n=2, grid 2x2, we need each row and column have exactly one uncovered square. So uncovered squares must be (1,1) and (2,2) (or swapped). Then we need to cover the other two squares (1,2) and (2,1). Could we cover them with one rectangle? The rectangle covering squares (1,2) and (2,1) would be shape of 2x2 minus diagonal, which is not a rectangle. So we need two rectangles: one covering (1,2) (a 1x1 rectangle) and one covering (2,1) (another 1x1 rectangle). So m=2 = n. So for n=2, we can use two 1x1 rectangles (tiles of size 1). That's allowed: rectangular tile can be 1x1. So each tile covers exactly one off-diagonal square. So m=n works.\n\nFor n=3, uncovered squares (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Off-diagonal squares are 6 squares. Can we cover them with 3 rectangles? Possibly each rectangle covers two squares? Let's try to find 3 rectangles covering all off-diagonal squares without covering any diagonal squares. Could we use rectangles: row1 columns 2-3 (1x2 rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3)), row2 columns 1 and 3 (but that's not contiguous). So maybe use column rectangles: column1 rows 2-3 (2x1 rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1)), column2 rows 1 and 3 (non-contiguous). So not possible. But we could use 1x1 tiles for each off-diagonal square, that's 6 tiles > n. But maybe we can use some 2x2 rectangles that avoid diagonal squares? Let's see: rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 2-3 includes squares (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). But (2,2) is diagonal, cannot include. So not allowed. So any rectangle covering more than one row and more than one column will inevitably include a diagonal square because if rectangle covers rows i and j (i<j) and columns p and q (p<q), then if i=p and j=q? Actually we need to avoid any (k,k) inside rectangle. If rectangle includes rows i..j and columns p..q, then if there exists k such that i ≤ k ≤ j and p ≤ k ≤ q, then (k,k) is inside rectangle. So to avoid diagonal squares, the rectangle's row interval and column interval must be disjoint: either all rows are less than all columns or all rows are greater than all columns. That is, the rectangle must be entirely above the diagonal (rows < columns) or below the diagonal (rows > columns). So a rectangle can be placed entirely in the upper triangular region (strictly above diagonal) or lower triangular region (strictly below