oint complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques). Each rectangle corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph between a set of rows and a set of columns. The condition that the rectangles are disjoint corresponds to the bicliques being edge-disjoint. So we need to partition the edges of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching into n bicliques. Is that possible? Let's think.\n\nWe have bipartite graph with bipartition (rows, columns). Edges correspond to squares (i,j). The missing edges are (i,i) for each i (the perfect matching). So we have a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching. We need to partition its edges into bicliques (complete bipartite subgraphs) that are edge-disjoint. The rank argument says at least n bicliques needed. The question: can we partition into exactly n bicliques? This is known as the biclique partition number of the complement of a perfect matching. I think the biclique partition number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is indeed n. There is a known decomposition: For each i, take biclique between rows {i} and columns {1,...,n} \\ {i}. That's exactly the row i minus column i. That's a biclique between a single row and n-1 columns. That's a rectangle of size 1 x (n-1). But as we noted, that is a rectangle (since it's a single row, columns contiguous? Wait, a rectangle defined by rows from i to i (single row) and columns from 1 to n except i. But the columns are not contiguous; they are two intervals: [1,i-1] and [i+1,n]. However, a rectangle in a grid is defined by a contiguous block of rows and columns. The set of squares where row index = i and column index ∈ S where S is any subset of columns, not necessarily contiguous, is not a rectangle unless S is a contiguous interval. So the rectangle must be contiguous in both dimensions. So the shape row i with missing column i is not a rectangle.\n\nThus the decomposition into n bicliques where each biclique corresponds to a rectangle must have contiguous column intervals. So the row i minus column i is not a rectangle. So the decomposition fails under the rectangle requirement.\n\nBut perhaps we can still partition into n rectangles by using rectangles that are not necessarily of height 1. For example, we could take rectangles that are \"staircase\" shapes? No, must be rectangles.\n\nLet's think about possible rectangle covering of K_{n,n} minus perfect matching. Each rectangle corresponds to a product of a set of rows (contiguous interval) and a set of columns (contiguous interval). So we need to partition the off-diagonal cells into such rectangles. The diagonal cells are excluded.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles needed. The rank lower bound is n, but the rectangle covering number (with contiguous intervals) might be larger.\n\nBut the problem statement: \"rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\" This implies each tile is a rectangle aligned with grid lines, i.e., a contiguous block of unit squares. So indeed each tile must be a rectangle.\n\nThus the solution's construction is invalid because each tile is not a rectangle (it has a missing column). So the answer 2025 may be incorrect. But maybe there is another construction with exactly 2025 rectangles that are contiguous? Let's explore.\n\nWe need to cover all off-diagonal squares. The uncovered squares are exactly one per row and column. Suppose we place uncovered squares on the main diagonal. Then we need to cover all squares (i,j) with i != j. Can we partition them into 2025 rectangles? Let's try to think of a decomposition.\n\nOne idea: For each k from 1 to n-1, consider the diagonal offset k: squares where j = i + k (mod n) maybe? But we need contiguous intervals.\n\nAlternatively, we could use rectangles of size (n-1) x 1 (vertical strips) that avoid the diagonal square. For each column j, we could take rectangle covering all rows except row j, i.e., rows 