hat includes cells both above and below diagonal but not the diagonal. But any rectangle that includes both sides of diagonal must cross the diagonal at some point, which would include a diagonal cell unless the rectangle is arranged such that the diagonal cells are at the corners? Let's think: Suppose we have a rectangle defined by rows a..b and columns c..d. The diagonal cells inside this rectangle are those where i=j and a ≤ i ≤ b, c ≤ i ≤ d. So if the intervals [a,b] and [c,d] intersect, then there will be diagonal cells (i,i) for i in intersection. So to avoid any diagonal cells, the row interval and column interval must be disjoint: either b < c (all rows less than all columns) or d < a (all rows greater than all columns). That is, the rectangle must lie entirely above the diagonal (rows < columns) or entirely below the diagonal (rows > columns). So each rectangle can only be in the strictly upper triangular region or strictly lower triangular region. So we cannot have a rectangle covering both sides without hitting diagonal.\n\nThus the problem reduces to partitioning the upper triangular region (i<j) into rectangles that lie entirely above diagonal, and similarly lower triangular region. The two regions are symmetric. So the minimal number of rectangles needed is at least the sum of minimal numbers needed for each region. But perhaps we can intermix them? No, they are disjoint sets, so we can treat them separately.\n\nThus we need to partition the strictly upper triangular matrix (size n(n-1)/2) into rectangles that are axis-aligned and lie above diagonal. Similarly for lower triangular region.\n\nNow the rank lower bound gave n. But that bound considered the whole matrix J - I, not each region separately. The rank of J - I is n, but perhaps we need at least n rectangles for the whole matrix, not necessarily n per region. Could we achieve n rectangles total by using some rectangles in upper region and some in lower region? Possibly we could use n rectangles: maybe n/2 in each region? But n is odd (2025). So maybe we can have (n+1)/2 in one region and (n-1)/2 in the other. But we need to check if possible.\n\nLet's try to find a decomposition of the off-diagonal matrix into n rectangles. This is known as the \"rectangle covering number\" (or \"biclique partition number\") of the complement of a perfect matching in a complete bipartite graph. The biclique partition number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is known to be n. Actually, I recall a known result: The bipartite dimension (or Boolean rank) of the matrix J - I is n. The Boolean rank is the minimum number of rank-1 binary matrices (outer products) needed to sum (over Boolean OR) to the matrix. However, in our case, we require the rank-1 matrices to be disjoint (i.e., sum over integers, not OR). But the Boolean rank doesn't require disjointness; overlapping is allowed as long as the OR yields the matrix. But we need disjointness because each square can be covered at most once. However, we could allow overlapping if we consider covering multiple times? The problem says at most one tile per unit square, so overlapping not allowed. So we need a partition, not just a covering.\n\nThe rank argument used integer addition, not OR, and required that the sum of the rank-1 matrices equals the matrix exactly, with each entry being either 0 or 1, and each rank-1 matrix has entries 0 or 1. Since the sum of these matrices yields entries 0 or 1, the matrices must be disjoint (no overlapping 1s). So indeed we need a partition into rank-1 binary matrices.\n\nThus the rank lower bound is n. The question: Is there a partition of J - I into n rank-1 binary matrices that are disjoint? That is exactly the problem of partitioning the complete bipartite graph minus a perfect matching into n edge-disjoint bicliques. I think there is a known construction: For each i, define biclique between rows {i} and columns {i+1,...,n} plus rows {i+1,...,n} and columns {i}? Wait, that would be two bic