\\le i\\le k\\}.\n  \\]\n  It covers the \\(k\\) cells above the hole in column \\(k+1\\).\n\nAll rectangles are axis\u2011parallel, their interiors are disjoint, and none contains a diagonal cell because every diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) lies either to the right of all \\(H_k\\) (when \\(i\\) is the row index) or below all \\(V_k\\) (when \\(i\\) is the column index).  \n\nEvery non\u2011diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies either \\(j<i\\) or \\(j>i\\):\n\n* If \\(j<i\\) then \\((i,j)\\in H_{j}\\) (the horizontal rectangle of row \\(i\\) with \\(k=j\\)).  \n* If \\(j>i\\) then \\((i,j)\\in V_{i}\\) (the vertical rectangle of column \\(j\\) with \\(k=i\\)).\n\nHence the \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles cover **all** cells except the diagonal ones, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered cell (its diagonal square).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Minimality\n\nThe lower bound of Section\u202f2 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, while the construction of Section\u202f3 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.** The smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place so that each row and each column of the \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid has exactly one uncovered unit square is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have reviewed the \u201cSummary\u201d and \u201cDetailed Solution\u201d sections. All statements are rigorously justified, every mathematical expression is enclosed in TeX delimiters, and the final answer follows the required \\boxed{} format. The solution is complete and adheres to all instructions."
