ersected by \\(T\\) form a consecutive interval, the same side (left or right) must be chosen for all of them; otherwise two rows would force opposite sides and the rectangle would have to contain a hole.  \nThus \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Corners\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) define the *left corner*  \n\n\\[\nc_i^{\\;L}=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) define the *right corner*  \n\n\\[\nc_i^{\\;R}=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\) and exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=n\\), the numbers of left and right corners are\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{left corners}\\}=n-1,\\qquad \n\\#\\{\\text{right corners}\\}=n-1.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the total number of corners is  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{corners}\\}=2n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  At most one corner per tile\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad \n\\text{A tile contained in }L\\text{ contains at most one left corner, and a tile contained in }R\\text{ contains at most one right corner.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof for }L.*  \nLet \\(T\\subseteq L\\) be a rectangle.  \nWrite its set of rows as a consecutive interval \\(I=\\{i_0,i_0+1,\\dots ,i_1\\}\\) and its column interval as \\([c,d]\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nSince \\(T\\subseteq L\\), for every \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(d<p(i)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nd<\\min_{i\\in I}p(i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(i^\\*\\in I\\) be the row for which \\(p(i^\\*)=\\min_{i\\in I}p(i)\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contains the left corner \\(c_{i^\\*}^{\\;L}=(i^\\*,p(i^\\*)-1)\\), then we must have  \n\n\\[\nd\\ge p(i^\\*)-1.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (8) yields \\(d=p(i^\\*)-1\\).  \nFor any other row \\(i\\in I\\setminus\\{i^\\*\\}\\) we have \\(p(i)>p(i^\\*)\\), whence  \n\n\\[\np(i)-1\\ge p(i^\\*)\\;>\\;d,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nso column \\(p(i)-1\\) (the left corner of row \\(i\\)) does **not** belong to \\(T\\).  \nThus \\(T\\) can contain the left corner of at most one row, namely the row with the smallest \\(p(i)\\) among its rows.  \n\nThe same argument applied to the right region \\(R\\) (using the condition \\(c> \\max_{i\\in I}p(i)\\)) shows that a rectangle in \\(R\\) can contain at most one right corner. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEvery tile is either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and, by Lemma\u202f2, contains at most one corner.  \nSince there are exactly \\(2n-2\\) corners (Equation\u202f(6)), any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThen the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Tiles for the left region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT^{L}_{j}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{L}_{j}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\) (a vertical strip).  \nAll squares of \\(L\\) belong to exactly one of the sets \\(T^{L}_{j}\\); the sets are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Tiles for the right region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT^{R}_{j}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{R}_{j}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles partition \\(R\\) and are disjoint from each other and from the left\u2011region tiles.\n\nThus we have exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \nEvery row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\); the squares to its left are covered by \\(T^{L}_{i-1}\\) (if \\(i>1\\)) and the squares to its right are covered by \\(T^{R}_{i+1}\\) (if \\(i<n\\)).  \nSimilarly, each column \\(j\\) has the uncovered square \\((j,j)\\) and all other squares of that column belong to exactly one of the tiles defined above.  \nHence the construction satisfies the required condition with precisely \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (10) and the explicit construction (15) coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
