            "content": "final answer.\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation model.**  \n   The uncovered squares form a set  \n   \\[\n   H=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n   \\]  \n   where \\(n=2025\\) and \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n\n2. **Two disjoint regions.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \n   R=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Every covered unit square belongs to exactly one of \\(L\\) or \\(R\\).  \n\n3. **Corners.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the square \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) is the *left corner* of \\(L\\); for each row with \\(p(i)<n\\) the square \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) is the *right corner* of \\(R\\).  \n   There are exactly \\(n-1\\) left corners and \\(n-1\\) right corners, i.e. \\(2n-2\\) corners in total.  \n\n4. **Upper‑bound on corners per tile.**  \n   *Lemma.* Any axis‑parallel rectangle that does not contain a hole lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\), and such a rectangle can contain **at most one** corner.  \n   Proof uses the fact that, for a rectangle in \\(L\\) with column interval \\([c,d]\\), we must have \\(d<\\min_{i\\in I}p(i)\\) where \\(I\\) is the set of its rows; consequently only the row with the smallest \\(p(i)\\) can contribute its left‑corner, and no other left‑corner can be inside the same rectangle. The same argument works for \\(R\\).  \n\n5. **Lower bound.**  \n   Since each tile can cover at most one corner and there are \\(2n-2\\) corners, any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical tile covering the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\).  \n   These are \\(n-1\\) tiles for the left region and \\(n-1\\) tiles for the right region, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles. They are disjoint, cover every square except the diagonal, and each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is tight; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let the unique uncovered square be \\((i,p(i))\\), where \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition into two regions\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs to exactly one of \\(L\\) or \\(R\\); the sets are disjoint because a square cannot satisfy simultaneously \\(j<p(i)\\) and \\(j>p(i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Tiles lie completely in one region\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad \n\\text{Every tile (axis‑parallel rectangle) that does not contain a hole is contained either in }L\\text{ or in }R.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a rectangle \\(T\\) that contains squares from two different rows, say rows \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contained a square left of \\((i,p(i))\\) and a square right of \\((i,p(i))\\) in the same row \\(i\\), then column \\(p(i)\\) would belong to \\(T\\), i.e. the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) would be covered – impossible.  \nHence, for each row intersected by \\(T\\), all squares of \\(T\\) lie either strictly left of the hole of that row or strictly right of it.  \nSince the rows intersected by \\(T\\) form a consecutive interval, the same side (left or right) must be chosen for all of them; otherwise two rows would force opposite sides and the rectangle would have to contain a hole.  \nThus \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Corners\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) define the *left corner*  \n\n\\[\nc_i^{\\;L}=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) define the *right corner*  \n\n\\[\nc_i^{\\;R}=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\) and exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=n\\), the numbers of left and right corners are\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{left corners}\\}=n-1,\\qquad \n\\#\\{\\text{right corners}\\}=n-1.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the total number of corners is  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{corners}\\}=2n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  At most one corner per tile\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad \n\\text{A tile contained in }L\\text{ contains at most one left corner, and a tile contained in }R\\text{ contains at most one right corner.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof for }L.*  \nLet \\(T\\subseteq L\\) be a rectangle.  \nWrite its set of rows as a consecutive interval \\(I=\\{i_0,i_0+1,\\dots ,i_1\\}\\) and its column interval as \\([c,d]\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nSince \\(T\\subseteq L\\), for every \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(d<p(i)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nd<\\min_{i\\in I}p(i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(i^\\*\\in I\\) be the row for which \\(p(i^\\*)=\\min_{i\\in I}p(i)\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contains the left corner \\(c_{i^\\*}^{\\;L}=(i^\\*,p(i^\\*)-1)\\), then we must have  \n\n\\[\nd\\ge p(i^\\*)-1.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (8) yields \\(d=p(i^\\*)-1\\).  \nFor any other row \\(i\\in I\\setminus\\{i^\\*\\}\\) we have \\(p(i)>p(i^\\*)\\), whence  \n\n\\[\np(i)-1\\ge p(i^\\*)\\;>\\;d,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nso column \\(p(i)-1\\) (the left corner of row \\(i\\)) does **not** belong to \\(T\\).  \nThus \\(T\\) can contain the left corner of at most one row, namely the row with the smallest \\(p(i)\\) among its rows.  \n\nThe same argument applied to the right region \\(R\\) (using the condition \\(c> \\max_{i\\in I}p(i)\\)) shows that a rectangle in \\(R\\) can contain at most one right corner. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEvery tile is either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\) (Lemma 1) and, by Lemma 2, contains at most one corner.  \nSince there are exactly \\(2n-2\\) corners (Equation (6)), any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThen the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Tiles for the left region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT^{L}_{j}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{L}_{j}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\) (a vertical strip).  \nAll squares of \\(L\\) belong to exactly one of the sets \\(T^{L}_{j}\\); the sets are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Tiles for the right region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT^{R}_{j}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{R}_{j}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles partition \\(R\\) and are disjoint from each other and from the left‑region tiles.\n\nThus we have exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \nEvery row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\); the squares to its left are covered by \\(T^{L}_{i-1}\\) (if \\(i>1\\)) and the squares to its right are covered by \\(T^{R}_{i+1}\\) (if \\(i<n\\)).  \nSimilarly, each column \\(j\\) has the uncovered square \\((j,j)\\) and all other squares of that column belong to exactly one of the tiles defined above.  \nHence the construction satisfies the required condition with precisely \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (10) and the explicit construction (15) coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
