tegers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is of the form  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor a row \\(i\\) the uncovered square lies in column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); the remaining squares in that row are the **left interval**  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\]\n\nand the **right interval**  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines and which covers a set of unit squares.  \nAll tiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and together must cover \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}(L_i\\cup R_i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (shape of a tile)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be a tile. Then either\n\n* all columns of \\(T\\) belong to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) for some \\(k\\le n-1\\) (a *left‑anchored* tile), or  \n* all columns of \\(T\\) belong to \\(\\{\\ell,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for some \\(\\ell\\ge2\\) (a *right‑anchored* tile).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider any row \\(i\\) that contains a cell of \\(T\\). In that row the uncovered square is at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); therefore the columns of \\(T\\) in row \\(i\\) cannot cross \\(\\sigma(i)\\). Hence either all columns of \\(T\\) in that row are \\(\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\) or all are \\(\\ge\\sigma(i)+1\\). Since \\(T\\) is a rectangle, the same set of columns occurs in every row of \\(T\\). Consequently either every column of \\(T\\) is \\(\\le\\min_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)-1\\) (where \\(I\\) is the set of rows of \\(T\\)), or every column of \\(T\\) is \\(\\ge\\max_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)+1\\). In the first case we may extend the rectangle leftwards to column 1 without meeting an uncovered square; in the second case we may extend it rightwards to column \\(n\\). Thus \\(T\\) is of one of the two described forms. ∎