ght-anchored. That seems correct.\n\nLemma 2: For each k from 1 to n-1, there exists a left-anchored tile whose rightmost column equals k. They argue: Since sigma is a permutation, there is a unique row r with sigma(r) = k+1. The cell (r,k) is in left interval L_r, must be covered by some tile T. Suppose T is right-anchored; then its column interval is [ell,n] with ell <= k (since it contains column k). Since the interval is contiguous, it also contains column k+1, which would cover (r,k+1), which is uncovered. Contradiction. So T must be left-anchored. Let its rightmost column be k_T. Since (r,k) in T, k_T >= k. If k_T > k, then column k+1 also belongs to T (since left-anchored tile's column interval is [1,k_T]), covering (r,k+1). Contradiction. So k_T = k. So there is a left-anchored tile with rightmost column k. That seems valid.\n\nSimilarly Lemma 3 for right-anchored tiles.\n\nThus at least n-1 left-anchored tiles and n-1 right-anchored tiles. Are there any possibilities that a tile could be both left-anchored and right-anchored? No, a tile cannot be both because left-anchored means columns <= k <= n-1, right-anchored means columns >= ell >=2. A tile could be both if its columns are within [1,n] but both conditions hold? Actually left-anchored means all columns are <= some k <= n-1. Right-anchored means all columns are >= some ell >=2. It's possible that a tile's columns are a subset of [2,n-1] and thus both left-anchored (with k = n-1) and right-anchored (with ell = 2). But the definitions in the solution are \"either all columns belong to {1,...,k} for some k <= n-1 (left-anchored) or all columns belong to {ell,...,n} for some ell >=2 (right-anchored)\". It doesn't say exclusive. But the lemmas rely on distinctness: they claim each left-anchored tile has a distinct rightmost column, and each right-anchored tile has a distinct leftmost column. Could a tile be counted in both categories? Possibly, but they claim distinctness because each k yields a distinct til