angle contained entirely in \\(\\mathcal L\\) or entirely in \\(\\mathcal R\\); a rectangle cannot intersect a uncovered square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for any tiling\n\n#### Lemma 1 (left region).  \nAt least \\(N-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\) there exists a unique row, call it \\(i_k\\), with \\(L_{i_k}=k\\) (because the values \\(L_i\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\)).  \nConsider the cell \\(p_k:=(i_k,k)\\). It is the rightmost cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) in its row.\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(p_k\\).  \nIf the rightmost column of \\(T\\) were larger than \\(k\\), then \\(T\\) would contain the square \\((i_k,k+1)\\), which is the uncovered square of row \\(i_k\\); this is impossible. Hence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nTwo distinct values \\(k\\neq k'\\) give rectangles with different rightmost columns, because a rectangle has a unique rightmost column. Consequently each \\(k\\) forces a distinct rectangle. Since there are \\(N-1\\) choices of \\(k\\), at least \\(N-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n#### Lemma 2 (right region).  \nAt least \\(N-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric. For each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(j_k\\) with \\(R_{j_k}=k\\) (i.e. the uncovered square of that row lies in column \\(N-k\\)).  \nThe cell \\(q_k:=(j_k,N-k)\\) is the leftmost cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\) in its row. Any rectangle covering \\(q_k\\) cannot extend to column \\(N-k-1\\), otherwise it would contain the uncovered square \\((j_k,N-k)\\). Hence the leftmost column of that rectangle is exactly \\(N-k\\). Distinct values of \\(k\\) yield rectangles with distinct leftmost columns, so at least \\(N-1\\) rectangles are necessary for \\(\\mathcal R\\). ∎\n\n