"**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of holes.**  \n   The condition \u201cexactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to be a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n2. **Left and right regions.**  \n   All covered squares split naturally into two disjoint regions:  \n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \n   R=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Any rectangle (tile) cannot contain a hole, hence it lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Key Lemma (right\u2011most squares).**  \n   In \\(L\\) consider the set of \u201cright\u2011most\u2019\u2019 squares  \n   \\[\n   S_L=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\mid \\sigma(i)>1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such squares, one in each column \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   **Lemma:** A rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** element of \\(S_L\\).  \n   *Proof idea:* If a rectangle occupies rows \\(I=[a,b]\\) and columns \\(J=[c,d]\\), then because it stays inside \\(L\\) we must have \\(d\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\); thus \\(\\sigma(i)\\ge d+1\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\). The only row whose hole lies exactly at column \\(d+1\\) is the unique \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)=d+1\\); only that row can contribute the square \\((i,d)\\in S_L\\). Hence at most one element of \\(S_L\\) lies in the rectangle.\n\n   Since \\(|S_L|=n-1\\), any partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   By symmetry the same argument applied to the set  \n   \\[\n   S_R=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\}\n   \\]  \n   (the \u201cleft\u2011most\u2019\u2019 squares of \\(R\\)) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\).\n\n4. **Lower bound.**  \n   Consequently any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]  \n   rectangles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - In \\(L\\) we have the staircase \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}\\).  For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) take the vertical rectangle covering rows \\(i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\) and column \\(j\\).  These are \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering \\(L\\).  \n   - In \\(R\\) we have the staircase \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}\\).  For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) take the vertical rectangle covering rows \\(i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\) and column \\(j\\).  Again we obtain \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering \\(R\\).\n\n   Altogether we have exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, meeting the lower bound.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of the unit squares  \n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square,\nthere exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the\nuncovered squares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles) whose\ninteriors are disjoint and which contain no point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Decomposition into left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nClearly \\(L\\cup R = B\\setminus U\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\nIf a rectangle contains a point of \\(L\\) and a point of \\(R\\) then it must\ncontain the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) of the row it meets, which is forbidden.\nHence **every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Right\u2011most squares of the left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) is\nthe right\u2011most square of that row inside \\(L\\).  Set  \n\\[\nS_L=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\mid \\sigma(i)>1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the column numbers\n\\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) run through \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) exactly once; therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_L|=n-1. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma: a rectangle in \\(L\\) contains at most one element of \\(S_L\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }R=I\\times J\\subseteq L\\text{ is a rectangle ( }I=[a,b],\\;J=[c,d]\\text{ ),\n then }|R\\cap S_L|\\le 1.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(R\\subseteq L\\), for every row \\(i\\in I\\) we have\n\\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) for all \\(j\\in J\\); in particular the rightmost column\n\\(d\\) of \\(J\\) satisfies \\(d\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\), i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)\\ge d+1\\).\nThus all hole columns of rows in \\(I\\) lie in the set \\(\\{d+1,d+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nIf a row \\(i\\in I\\) contributes a square of \\(S_L\\) to the rectangle,\nthat square must be \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in J\\); hence \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\le d\\).\nCombined with \\(\\sigma(i)\\ge d+1\\) we obtain \\(\\sigma(i)=d+1\\) and the\nsquare is \\((i,d)\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, there is **at most one** row with\n\\(\\sigma(i)=d+1\\).  Consequently \\(R\\) can contain at most one element of\n\\(S_L\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Consequence for the left region  \n\nEvery rectangle used to tile \\(L\\) contains at most one element of \\(S_L\\);\nby (4) the set \\(S_L\\) has \\(n-1\\) elements, each of which must be covered.\nHence any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Symmetric statement for the right region  \n\nDefine the \u201cleft\u2011most\u2019\u2019 squares of \\(R\\) by  \n\\[\nS_R=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nExactly the same argument (mirroring left/right) shows that a rectangle\ncontained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one element of \\(S_R\\), and that\n\\(|S_R|=n-1\\).  Therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to\ncover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Global lower bound  \n\nSince every tile belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\), the total number of\ntiles is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Covering \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_c^{\\;L}= \\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all of\n\\(L\\).\n\n*Covering \\(R\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) take the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_c^{\\;R}= \\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain we obtain \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering \\(R\\).\n\nThus the whole board (except the diagonal holes) is tiled with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (6) and the explicit construction coincide; therefore\n\\(2n-2\\) is the minimum possible number of tiles.\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
