he set of cells just below diagonal. So we need at least n-1 rectangles to cover all those n-1 cells. Similarly, for the cells just above diagonal (i,i+1) for i=1..n-1, each rectangle can contain at most one of those cells. So we need at least n-1 rectangles for above region. So total at least 2n-2. This is a valid lower bound argument using a fooling set of size 2n-2 (the set of cells just above and below diagonal). The solution's description of the fooling set is slightly inaccurate but the underlying idea is correct: each rectangle can contain at most one of those cells. The solution's statement \"for any two distinct positions (r1,c1),(r2,c2) in F we have either r1=c2 or r2=c1\" is not correct",
