                            "text": "We have a problem: \"Consider a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nSolution: The solution uses a reduction to diagonal case, then uses Boolean rank and fooling set to get lower bound 2n-2, then constructs tiling with 2n-2 rectangles: For each column j (1 to n-1) a vertical rectangle covering squares below diagonal; for each column j (2 to n) a vertical rectangle covering squares above diagonal. That yields total 2n-2 rectangles. So answer is 4048.\n\nWe need to verify answer correctness, reasoning validity, and presentation quality.\n\nWe must follow hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, Level 1: Answer correctness.\n\nThe answer: 4048. Is that correct? Let's think: The problem asks for minimum number of tiles needed such that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square. The solution claims minimal number is 2n-2 = 2*2025 - 2 = 4048. We need to verify if that answer is indeed correct. Let's think about the problem.\n\nWe have a 2025x2025 grid. We need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned) covering unit squares, each tile can be any size (including 1x1?), but each unit square can be covered at most once. Each row and each column must have exactly one uncovered unit square. So there are 2025 rows and 2025 columns, each must have exactly one uncovered square, which means there are exactly 2025 uncovered squares, one per row and column. That means the uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: each row has exactly one uncovered square, each column has exactly one uncovered square. So the uncovered squares are at positions (i, sigma(i)) for some permutation sigma.\n\nGoal: minimize number of tiles covering the rest of the squares (the complement of the uncovered squares). Tiles can be any rectangles covering only covered squares (i.e., they cannot include uncovered squares). Each tile must be axis-aligned, covering a rectangular region of unit squares. Tiles cannot overlap (since each unit square can be covered at most once). So we need to partition the set of covered squares into rectangles (tiles). The uncovered squares are holes, each tile cannot cover a hole.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., all entries except a set of n positions) with axis-aligned rectangles, disjoint, covering all other squares.\n\nGoal: minimize number of rectangles needed.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles needed to tile the board minus the permutation matrix.\n\nThe solution claims minimal number is 2n-2.\n\nIs that correct? Let's think.\n\nWe can think of the board as an n x n matrix with n holes (the permutation). The complement is all other entries. We need to partition them into rectangles that avoid the holes. The holes are placed one per row and column. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the complement is known to be 2n - O(1). Actually, there is known result: The Boolean rank of J - I (the all-ones matrix minus identity) is n. Wait, the Boolean rank of J - I is known to be n? Let's recall. J is all-ones matrix, I is identity. J - I is matrix with zeros on diagonal, ones elsewhere. The Boolean rank of J - I is known to be n. Actually, I recall that the Boolean rank of J - I is n. Let's check: The Boolean rank is the smallest number r such that the matrix can be expressed as OR of r rank-1 binary matrices (i.e., outer products of binary vectors). This is exactly the minimal number of all-1 submatrices needed to cover all 1 entries. For J - I, the Boolean rank is known to be n. But the solution claims 2n-2, which is larger. Let's verify.\n\nWait, there is a known result: The Boolean rank of J - I is n. Let's verify: J - I has zeros on diagonal, ones elsewhere. The Boolean rank is the minimum number of rank-1 binary matrices (outer products of binary vectors) whose OR gives the matrix. Each rank-1 binary matrix corresponds to a rectangle (all-1 submatrix). So the minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all 1 entries (i.e., all off-diagonal entries) is the Boolean rank. Known results: The Boolean rank of J - I is n. Indeed, there is a known decomposition: For each i from 1 to n, define u_i = e_i + all-ones vector? Actually, we need to cover all off-diagonal entries. Let's try to construct a covering with n rectangles: For each i, take rectangle covering row i (i.e., the entire row) except the diagonal entry. But a rectangle must be contiguous block of rows and columns. Row i is a 1 x n rectangle; but we cannot include the diagonal entry (i,i). So we cannot take the whole row as a rectangle because it includes the diagonal. But we could take two rectangles per row: left part (columns 1..i-1) and right part (columns i+1..n). That would be 2 per row, total 2n. But we can combine across rows: For each column j, we can take a rectangle covering all rows except the diagonal? But that also includes diagonal entries. So we need to avoid each diagonal entry.\n\nLet's think about covering the off-diagonal entries with rectangles that avoid diagonal entries. The solution's construction uses vertical rectangles for each column: For column j, we cover rows > j (below diagonal) as one rectangle, and rows < j (above diagonal) as another rectangle. So for each column j, there are two rectangles (except maybe first and last column). Actually they have n-1 rectangles for below diagonal (columns 1..n-1) and n-1 for above diagonal (columns 2..n). So total 2n-2 rectangles. That covers all off-diagonal entries. Indeed, each column j (1..n-1) has rectangle covering rows j+1..n (below diagonal). Each column j (2..n) has rectangle covering rows 1..j-1 (above diagonal). So each column j has two rectangles except the first column (only below) and last column (only above). So total 2n-2.\n\nIs it possible to cover the off-diagonal entries with fewer than 2n-2 rectangles? The solution uses a fooling set of size 2n-2 to prove lower bound. But is that fooling set valid? Let's examine.\n\nThe fooling set defined is F = {(i,i+1) for i=1..n-1} ∪ {(i+1,i) for i=1..n-1}. That's the entries just above and just below the diagonal. So there are 2(n-1) entries. The property: For any two distinct positions in F, either they share a row or column, and the other position (the cross) is a diagonal zero. Indeed, if we take (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i ≠ j, then they are in different rows and columns (i vs j, i+1 vs j+1). The cross entries are (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). Are any of those diagonal? Not necessarily. But the claim is that for any two distinct positions in F, we have either r1 = c2 or r2 = c1. Let's check: For (i,i+1) and (i+1,i), we have r1 = i, c1 = i+1; r2 = i+1, c2 = i. Then r1 = c2 (i = i) and r2 = c1 (i+1 = i+1). So cross entries are diagonal zeros: a_{i,i} = 0, a_{i+1,i+1} = 0. For (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i ≠ j, we have r1 = i, c1 = i+1; r2 = j, c2 = j+1. Then r1 = c2? i = j+1? Not generally. r2 = c1? j = i+1? Not generally. So the property fails for nonadjacent pairs. However, the definition of fooling set for Boolean rank is: A set of positions (i_k, j_k) such that for any k ≠ l, the entry at (i_k, j_l) is 0 (or the entry at (i_l, j_k) is 0). Actually, the definition: A fooling set is a set of 1-entries such that for any two distinct entries (i, j) and (k, l) in the set, either a_{i,l} = 0 or a_{k,j} = 0. This ensures that any rank-1 matrix (outer product) can cover at most one of the entries in the set. So the lower bound is the size of the largest fooling set.\n\nNow, does the set F satisfy that property? Let's test for (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i < j-1 (i.e., not adjacent). Then (i, j+1) is off-diagonal? Since i ≠ j+1 (i < j-1 < j+1), it's not diagonal. So a_{i,j+1} = 1. Similarly, a_{j,i+1} = 1. So both cross entries are 1, not 0. So the condition fails. So F is not a fooling set unless we restrict to only adjacent pairs? Actually the set includes all pairs (i,i+1) and (i+1,i). The cross entries for two distinct pairs might be non-diagonal. Let's examine all possible pairs:\n\n- Pair (i,i+1) and (i+1,i): cross entries are (i,i) and (i+1,i+1) which are zeros. Good.\n\n- Pair (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i ≠ j: cross entries are (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). Are any of these diagonal? Only if i = j+1 or j = i+1. That would happen if i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1. That would be i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1. So i = j+1 => i = j+1 => i = j+1. That means i = j+1, i.e., i = j+1 => i > j. Similarly j = i+1 => j > i. So for pairs where indices differ by 1, we have cross entries diagonal. For pairs where indices differ by more than 1, cross entries are off-diagonal (1). So the set F is not a fooling set of size 2n-2. However, perhaps the solution's definition of fooling set is not rigorous: they claim that for any two distinct positions in F, we have either r1 = c2 or r2 = c1. But that's false for nonadjacent pairs. Let's check: For (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i < j-1, we have r1 = i, c1 = i+1; r2 = j, c2 = j+1. r1 = c2? i = j+1? No. r2 = c1? j = i+1? No. So the condition fails. So the claim is false.\n\nThus the lower bound argument is flawed. However, maybe they intended a different fooling set: The set of all off-diagonal entries of the form (i,i+1) and (i+1,i) for each i yields 2(n-1) entries. But the fooling set condition fails for nonadjacent pairs. So the lower bound is not valid. However, there might be a different known lower bound: The Boolean rank of J - I is indeed n. Actually, I recall that the Boolean rank of J - I is n. Let's verify known results: The Boolean rank of the matrix with zeros on diagonal and ones elsewhere is n. Indeed, there is a known result: The Boolean rank of the complement of the identity matrix (i.e., J - I) is n. This can be seen because the matrix has rank n over the reals, but Boolean rank can be less. However, I think the Boolean rank of J - I is n, not 2n-2. Let's check known results: The Boolean rank of the matrix J - I (the adjacency matrix of the complete graph minus loops) is known to be n. Actually, the Boolean rank of the adjacency matrix of a complete graph (with zeros on diagonal) is known to be n. Let's recall: For the complete graph K_n, the adjacency matrix is J - I. The Boolean rank of the adjacency matrix of K_n is known to be n. Because the adjacency matrix can be expressed as the OR of n rank-1 matrices: For each vertex i, define u_i = e_i (the i-th unit vector), v_i = all-ones vector except maybe something? Actually, we can cover each off-diagonal entry by n rectangles: For each i, take rectangle covering row i (all columns) and column i (all rows) but we need to avoid diagonal. Let's think: For each i, we can take rectangle covering row i (i.e., the set of entries (i,j) for all j) and rectangle covering column i (i.e., entries (j,i) for all j). But each of those includes the diagonal (i,i). However, we can combine row i and column i into two rectangles that avoid diagonal? Actually, we can take rectangle covering row i and columns except i: That's not a rectangle because you need contiguous columns. So we can't exclude a column from a rectangle. So we need to break each row into two rectangles: left of diagonal and right of diagonal. That's exactly the solution's construction: 2n-2 rectangles. So perhaps the Boolean rank is indeed 2n-2? Let's check known results: The Boolean rank of J - I is known to be n? Let's verify with small n.\n\nFor n=2: J - I = [[0,1],[1,0]]. Off-diagonal entries are two 1's. Minimal rectangles covering them: each 1 is isolated because they are not in same row or column? Actually they are in different rows and columns, but they are not in same row or column? They are at positions (1,2) and (2,1). They cannot be covered by a single rectangle because a rectangle covering both would include (1,1) and (2,2) which are zeros. So we need at least 2 rectangles. So Boolean rank is 2 for n=2. 2n-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nFor n=3: J - I = [[0,1,1],[1,0,1],[1,1,0]]. Off-diagonal entries are 6 ones. Can we cover them with fewer than 4 rectangles? 2n-2 = 4. Let's try to cover with 3 rectangles? Let's see if possible. Each rectangle is a submatrix of ones. The matrix is all ones except diagonal. Could we cover all off-diagonal entries with 3 rectangles? Let's try to find a covering: For each i, we can take rectangle covering rows i and columns all except i? That would be a rectangle of shape (1 x n) but missing column i. Not allowed. So we need to break each row into two rectangles: left and right of diagonal. That yields 2 per row, total 6 rectangles. But we can combine across rows? For example, rectangle covering rows 1..2 and columns 3..3 (i.e., column 3 rows 1-2) covers entries (1,3) and (2,3). That's okay. Similarly, rectangle covering rows 2..3 and column 1 covers (2,1) and (3,1). And rectangle covering rows 1..3 and column 2? That includes (2,2) which is zero. So not allowed. Let's try to find a covering with 4 rectangles: The solution's construction uses vertical rectangles: For each column j, one rectangle covering rows > j (below diagonal) and one covering rows < j (above diagonal). For n=3, we have columns 1,2,3. For column 1, rectangle covering rows 2..3 (below diagonal) => covers (2,1),(3,1). For column 2, rectangle covering rows 1..1 (above) => (1,2); and rectangle covering rows 3..3 (below) => (3,2). For column 3, rectangle covering rows 1..2 (above) => (1,3),(2,3). That's total rectangles: column 1: 1 rectangle; column 2: 2 rectangles; column 3: 1 rectangle => total 4 rectangles. So 2n-2 = 4 works.\n\nCould we cover with 3 rectangles? Let's try to find a covering: Suppose we take rectangle covering rows 1..2 and columns 2..3: This includes entries (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). But (2,2) is zero, not allowed. So can't include diagonal. So any rectangle covering more than one row and more than one column will inevitably include a diagonal entry if the rectangle includes any row i and column i. So to avoid diagonal entries, any rectangle must be such that its set of rows and columns are disjoint (i.e., no row index equals any column index in the rectangle). Because if a rectangle includes a row i and a column i, then the cell (i,i) is included, which is a zero. So each rectangle must have row set R and column set C such that R ∩ C = ∅. So each rectangle is a bipartite submatrix with rows and columns disjoint. So we need to partition the off-diagonal entries into rectangles where rows and columns are disjoint. This is essentially a bipartite covering problem.\n\nThus the minimal number of rectangles needed is the size of a minimum edge covering of the complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching (the diagonal). Actually, the off-diagonal entries correspond to edges in a complete bipartite graph between left vertices (rows) and right vertices (columns) where we exclude the perfect matching edges (i,i). So we have a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching. We need to cover all edges with complete bipartite subgraphs (i.e., rectangles) that are edge-disjoint (since each unit square can be covered at most once). Actually, we need a partition of edges into complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques) that are edge-disjoint. This is known as the biclique partition number of the complement of a perfect matching in K_{n,n}. The biclique partition number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is known to be 2n-2. Indeed, there is known result: The bipartite dimension (minimum number of complete bipartite subgraphs needed to partition the edges of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching) is n-1? Let's recall known results: The bipartite dimension of the complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} is 1 (the whole graph). The bipartite dimension of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is known to be n? Actually, there is known result: The bipartite dimension of the complement of a perfect matching in K_{n,n} is n-1? Let's check.\n\nWe need to partition edges of K_{n,n} \\ M (where M is a perfect matching) into complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques). This is known as the bipartite edge covering number or biclique partition number. There is known result: The biclique partition number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is n-1? Let's test for small n.\n\nn=2: K_{2,2} minus perfect matching (i.e., remove edges (1,1) and (2,2)?) Actually K_{2,2} has edges between left vertices {1,2} and right vertices {1,2}. Perfect matching edges are (1,1) and (2,2). Removing them leaves edges (1,2) and (2,1). These two edges cannot be covered by a single biclique because any biclique that includes both edges would need left vertices {1,2} and right vertices {1,2}, which includes (1,1) and (2,2) which are missing. So we need at least 2 bicliques. So biclique partition number is 2. 2n-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nn=3: K_{3,3} minus perfect matching leaves 6 edges. Partition into bicliques: The solution uses 4 bicliques. Could we do with 3? Let's try to find a partition of 6 edges into 3 bicliques. Each biclique is a complete bipartite subgraph between a set of left vertices L and right vertices R, with L ∩ R = ∅ (since we cannot include diagonal edges). Actually, the condition that L ∩ R = ∅ is necessary to avoid diagonal edges. So each biclique must be a bipartite subgraph between left vertices and right vertices that are disjoint sets. So each biclique corresponds to a rectangle in the matrix where rows and columns are disjoint.\n\nWe need to partition the edges of K_{3,3} minus perfect matching. Let's label left vertices (rows) 1,2,3 and right vertices (columns) 1',2',3' (or just columns 1,2,3). The edges present are all pairs (i,j) with i ≠ j. So edges: (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). We need to partition them into bicliques with disjoint row and column sets. Let's try to find 3 bicliques:\n\n- Biclique A: L = {1}, R = {2,3} => edges (1,2),(1,3). Good.\n\n- Biclique B: L = {2}, R = {1,3} => edges (2,1),(2,3). Good.\n\n- Biclique C: L = {3}, R = {1,2} => edges (3,1),(3,2). Good.\n\nThat's 3 bicliques. But note that each biclique is a rectangle of size 1 x 2. Are they disjoint? Yes, each edge appears exactly once. So we have a partition into 3 bicliques. So the biclique partition number is at most 3. The solution's construction uses 4 bicliques (vertical rectangles). So perhaps we can do better than 2n-2 for n=3. Indeed, we found a partition with 3 bicliques, which is less than 4. So the lower bound 2n-2 is not valid for n=3. Wait, but the solution's construction uses vertical rectangles that are column-based: For each column j, rectangle covering rows > j (below) and rows < j (above). For n=3, that yields 4 rectangles: column 1: rows 2-3 (2 entries); column 2: rows 1 (above) and rows 3 (below) => two rectangles; column 3: rows 1-2 (above) => one rectangle. So 4 rectangles. But we found a covering with 3 rectangles: each row's left and right sides combined into a single rectangle per row? Actually we used rectangles per row: Row 1: columns 2,3 (two entries) => rectangle (row 1, columns 2-3) is allowed because row set {1} and column set {2,3} are disjoint. Row 2: columns 1,3 => rectangle (row 2, columns 1,3) is not contiguous because columns 1 and 3 are not contiguous (there's column 2 in between). But a rectangle must be contiguous in both dimensions: It must be a product of a set of consecutive rows and a set of consecutive columns? Actually, in grid tiling, a rectangle is defined as a set of unit squares whose sides lie on grid lines, i.e., a contiguous block of rows and columns. So the rectangle must be a contiguous block of rows and a contiguous block of columns. So a rectangle covering row 2 and columns {1,3} is not allowed because columns are not contiguous (there's a gap at column 2). However, we could cover row 2 with two rectangles: one covering (2,1) and one covering (2,3). But we need to partition all edges into rectangles that are contiguous blocks. So the covering we gave earlier is not allowed because rectangle (row 2, columns 1,3) is not contiguous. So we need to consider contiguity.\n\nThus the problem is not just about bicliques; it's about axis-aligned rectangles (contiguous). So the covering must be by submatrices that are contiguous in both rows and columns. So the solution's vertical rectangles are contiguous: each rectangle is a column segment (width 1, height contiguous). That's allowed. So the lower bound might be correct under contiguity constraints.\n\nBut is there a covering with fewer than 2n-2 rectangles using contiguous rectangles? Let's explore.\n\nWe have an n x n grid with holes on diagonal positions (i,i). We need to cover all other squares with axis-aligned rectangles that avoid the diagonal holes. Each rectangle must be contiguous block of rows and columns. The diagonal holes block any rectangle that includes both row i and column i. So any rectangle cannot include a cell where row index equals column index. So any rectangle must have its row set and column set disjoint. Moreover, the rectangle must be contiguous: rows must be a consecutive interval [r1, r2], columns must be a consecutive interval [c1, c2], and the rectangle includes all cells (i,j) with r1 ≤ i ≤ r2, c1 ≤ j ≤ c2. The condition that row set and column set are disjoint implies that either the entire row interval lies entirely above the column interval (i.e., r2 < c1) or entirely below (c2 < r1). Because if intervals overlap, there would be some i in rows and j in columns such that i = j (if intervals intersect). Actually, if intervals [r1, r2] and [c1, c2] intersect, there exists some integer k that belongs to both intervals. Then cell (k,k) is in the rectangle, which is a hole. So not allowed. Therefore any rectangle must have row interval completely to the left of column interval (i.e., r2 < c1) or row interval completely to the right of column interval (c2 < r1). In other words, the rectangle must lie entirely in the region above the diagonal (i.e., rows less than columns) or below the diagonal (rows greater than columns). So each rectangle is either in the region L = {i > j} (below diagonal) or region R = {i < j} (above diagonal). So we have two separate regions: lower triangular (strictly below diagonal) and upper triangular (strictly above diagonal). The rectangles cannot cross the diagonal.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the lower triangular region (including all squares where row > column) and the upper triangular region (row < column) separately with axis-aligned rectangles. The lower triangular region is a shape of a right triangle (including squares below diagonal). Similarly for upper triangular region.\n\nNow, we need to partition each triangular region into rectangles. The solution uses vertical strips: For each column j, the rectangle covering rows j+1..n (below diagonal) is a vertical rectangle of width 1 (column j) and height n - j. Similarly for above diagonal, for each column j, rectangle covering rows 1..j-1 (above diagonal) is a vertical rectangle of width 1 (column j) and height j-1. So each column yields up to two rectangles (one above, one below). The total number of rectangles is (n-1) + (n-1) = 2n-2. This is a valid covering.\n\nBut could we reduce the number of rectangles by using larger rectangles that span multiple columns? Let's consider covering the lower triangular region with rectangles that span multiple columns. However, any rectangle spanning multiple columns must have row interval entirely below column interval. For a rectangle covering columns from c1 to c2 (c1 ≤ c2), the row interval must be a contiguous interval [r1, r2] such that r1 > c2 (since rows must be > columns). So the rectangle must be entirely below the diagonal, with rows starting at a number greater than the maximum column index in the rectangle. So for a rectangle covering columns c1..c2, the rows must be from some r1 > c2 to r2 (≥ r1). So the rectangle's rows are all > c2, meaning the rectangle is a block in the lower-left corner of the matrix, but not crossing the diagonal.\n\nThus we can think of covering the lower triangular region with rectangles that are anchored at the bottom-left corner? Actually, not anchored at bottom-left necessarily; they can be anywhere as long as rows > columns.\n\nSimilarly, for upper triangular region, rectangles must have column interval > row interval.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the region below diagonal (strictly) with rectangles that are \"staircase\" shaped: each rectangle is a block where the row interval is above the column interval (i.e., rows start after columns). The shape of the region below diagonal is a triangle. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a triangular region of size n x n (strictly lower triangular) with axis-aligned rectangles is known to be n-1. Indeed, we can partition the lower triangular region into n-1 rectangles by taking vertical strips as done: for each column j from 1 to n-1, rectangle covering rows j+1..n. That's n-1 rectangles. Could we do fewer? Let's think.\n\nThe lower triangular region consists of cells (i,j) with i > j. This region can be partitioned into rectangles in many ways. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a staircase shape (triangular shape) is known to be n-1. Actually, there is known result: The rectangle covering number of a Young diagram shape (like a triangular shape) equals the number of rows (or columns) of the diagram? Let's recall: For a Ferrers diagram shape, the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition it is equal to the number of \"corners\" or something. For a triangular shape (strictly lower triangular), the shape has n-1 rows (row lengths: n-1, n-2, ..., 1). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition this shape is n-1. Indeed, one can partition by taking each row as a rectangle (horizontal strips) or each column as a rectangle (vertical strips). But each row is a rectangle of width equal to row index? Wait, row i (i from 2 to n) has columns 1..i-1 (i-1 cells). That's a contiguous block of columns (1..i-1). So each row can be a rectangle of height 1 (row i) and width i-1. So we could partition the lower triangular region into n-1 rectangles (one per row). Similarly, we could partition into n-1 rectangles (one per column). So the minimal number is at most n-1. Could it be less than n-1? Let's test for small n.\n\nn=2: lower triangular region has 1 cell (2,1). Minimum rectangles needed is 1. n-1 = 1. So matches.\n\nn=3: lower triangular region has cells (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). That's shape: row 2: one cell at column 1; row 3: two cells at columns 1,2. Could we cover with 1 rectangle? No, because a rectangle covering (2,1) and (3,2) would also include (2,2) which is diagonal (hole). So not allowed. Could we cover with 2 rectangles? Possibly: rectangle covering column 1 rows 2-3 (vertical strip) covers (2,1),(3,1); rectangle covering row 3 column 2 (single cell) covers (3,2). That's 2 rectangles. So n-1 = 2. So minimal is 2.\n\nn=4: lower triangular region has rows: row2: col1; row3: col1-2; row4: col1-3. Could we partition into 2 rectangles? Let's try: rectangle covering column1 rows2-4 (vertical) covers (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Then rectangle covering rows3-4 columns2-3? That would be rows 3-4, columns 2-3, includes (3,2),(3,3),(4,2),(4,3). But (3,3) and (4,4) are diagonal? Actually (3,3) is diagonal, but rectangle includes (3,3) which is not allowed. So can't. Could we partition into 3 rectangles? Possibly: vertical column1 rows2-4; vertical column2 rows3-4; vertical column3 row4. That's 3 rectangles (n-1). So minimal seems to be n-1.\n\nThus the lower triangular region requires at least n-1 rectangles. Similarly, the upper triangular region also requires at least n-1 rectangles. So total at least 2n-2. So the lower bound of 2n-2 is plausible.\n\nBut is there a proof that lower triangular region requires at least n-1 rectangles? The solution uses a fooling set of size 2n-2 to prove lower bound for the whole matrix. However, the fooling set argument seems flawed as we saw. But perhaps there is a simpler argument: each rectangle can cover at most one cell in each \"anti-diagonal\"? Actually, we can argue that each rectangle can cover at most one cell from each \"diagonal\" of the shape? Let's think.\n\nAlternatively, we can argue that each rectangle in the lower triangular region can cover at most one cell from each row? Actually, rectangle can cover many cells in a row, but maybe each rectangle can cover at most one \"corner\"? Let's think.\n\nBetter approach: For the lower triangular region, consider the set of cells (i,i-1) for i = 2..n (the cells just below the diagonal). These are n-1 cells. In any rectangle covering cells in the lower triangular region, if it includes two of these cells (i,i-1) and (j,j-1) with i < j, then the rectangle would need to include rows i..j and columns i-1..j-1. Since i-1 < i, the column interval includes i, which is equal to row i, leading to diagonal cell (i,i) being included, which is not allowed. Actually, let's verify: Suppose rectangle includes (i,i-1) and (j,j-1) with i < j. Then the rectangle's row interval must include rows i..j, and column interval must include columns i-1..j-1 (since those are the column indices of the two cells). Then the rectangle includes cell (i,i) because row i is in row interval and column i is in column interval (since column interval includes i because i-1 ≤ i ≤ j-1). Indeed, i is between i-1 and j-1 inclusive. So (i,i) is included, which is a hole. So any rectangle can contain at most one cell from th