\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{U}= \\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal{U}\\) is a permutation matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Matrix representation of the covering\n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \na_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered},\\\\[2pt]\n0 & \\text{if }(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{U}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(J\\) be the all‑ones matrix and let \\(P_\\sigma\\) be the permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. \\((P_\\sigma)_{ij}=1\\) iff \\(j=\\sigma(i)\\)).  \nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\nA = J-P_\\sigma .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Each tile contributes a rank‑1 matrix\n\nConsider a rectangular tile \\(T\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nR_T=\\{i\\mid\\text{row }i\\text{ is used by }T\\},\\qquad \nC_T=\\{j\\mid\\text{column }j\\text{ is used by }T\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a tile is a Cartesian product of a set of consecutive rows and a set of consecutive columns, its indicator matrix is  \n\n\\[\nM_T=(m_{ij})_{i,j},\\qquad \nm_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if }i\\in R_T\\text{ and }j\\in C_T,\\\\\n0 & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(u_T\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) for the row‑indicator vector (\\((u_T)_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in R_T\\)) and \\(v_T\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) for the column‑indicator vector, we have  \n\n\\[\nM_T = u_T\\,v_T^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus each tile contributes a matrix of rank at most 1 (rank 0 only if the tile is empty, which never occurs).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nSuppose Matilda uses \\(k\\) tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_k\\).  \nSince the tiles are disjoint, the matrix of covered squares is the sum of their indicator matrices:\n\n\\[\nA = \\sum_{t=1}^{k} M_{T_t}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3), each \\(M_{T_t}\\) has rank \\(\\le 1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le \\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{T_t})\\le k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence a lower bound on \\(k\\) is \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).\n\nWe now compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).  \nBecause a permutation matrix is invertible, \\(P_\\sigma\\) is nonsingular.  \nMoreover \\(J\\) is invariant under any permutation: \\(P_\\sigma J = J P_\\sigma = J\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nI_\\sigma - J = P_\\sigma\\,(I-J)\\,P_\\sigma^{-1},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\operatorname{rank}(I_\\sigma-J)=\\operatorname{rank}(I-J)\\).  \nThe matrix \\(I-J\\) has eigenvalues  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_1 = 1-n\\quad\\text{(with eigenvector }\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}),\\qquad \n\\lambda_2=\\dots =\\lambda_n = 1\\quad\\text{(on the subspace orthogonal to }\\mathbf{1}).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\det(I-J)=1-n\\neq0\\) for \\(n>1\\); consequently \\(I-J\\) is invertible and \\(\\operatorname{rank}(I-J)=n\\).  \nSince \\(A = J-P_\\sigma = -(I_\\sigma-J)\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence **any** tiling satisfying the conditions needs at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nChoose any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) (for concreteness take the identity \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\)).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single rectangular tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq \\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThe tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.  \nEvery row contains exactly one uncovered square (by construction), and each column \\(j\\) contains exactly the uncovered square \\((\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)\\).  \nThus the t