e matrix is invertible and has full rank \\(n\\). \u220e  \n   - Combining (1), the lemma, and (2) yields the lower bound  \n     \\[\n     k\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n.\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   - Choose any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) (for concreteness take \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\)).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) place a single tile covering all squares of that row except the square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  The tile is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle, perfectly allowed.  \n   - The \\(n\\) tiles lie in distinct rows, so they are disjoint.  \n   - By construction each row \\(i\\) has exactly one uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\); each column \\(j\\) contains exactly the uncovered square \\((\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)\\). Thus the required condition is satisfied with exactly \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The lower bound (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles.  \n   - The explicit construction uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles.  \n   - Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the \\(n\\times n\\) grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Encoding the covering\n\nDefine the matrix  \n\\[\nA=(a_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \na_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if the unit square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered},\\\\[2pt]\n0 &\\text{if }(i,j)\\text{ is uncovered}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\n\\mathcal U=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(P_\\sigma\\) be the corresponding permutation matrix, i.e. \\((P_\\sigma)_{ij}=1\\) iff \\(j=\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise.  Let \\(J\\) denote the all\u2011ones matrix.  Then\n\\[\nA = J - P_\\sigma . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Tiles as rank\u20111 matrices\n\nConsider a rectangular tile \\(T\\).  Let\n\\[\nR_T=\\{i\\mid\\text{row }i\\text{ is occupied by }T\\},\\qquad \nC_T=\\{j\\mid\\text{column }j\\text{ is occupied by }T\\}.\n\\]\nBecause a tile is a Cartesian product of a set of consecutive rows and a set of consecutive columns, its indicator matrix is\n\\[\nM_T=(m_{ij}),\\qquad \nm_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }i\\in R_T\\text{ and }j\\in C_T,\\\\\n0 &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nDefine the row\u2011indicator vector \\(u_T\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) by \\((u_T)_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in R_T\\), and the column\u2011indicator vector \\(v_T\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) analogously.  Then\n\\[\nM_T = u_T\\,v_T^{\\mathsf T}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nHence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M_T)\\le 1\\).\n\nIf Matilda uses \\(k\\) tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_k\\), the covering matrix is the sum of their indicator matrices (the tiles are disjoint, so no entry exceeds\u202f1):\n\\[\nA = \\sum_{t=1}^{k} M_{T_t}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nUsing the subadditivity of rank,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le \\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{T_t})\\le k. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible tiling must satisfy\n\\[\nk\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(A). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\)\n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n**Lemma.** For any permutation matrix \\(P_\\sigma\\) with \\(n>1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P_\\sigma)=n.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Suppose \\((J-P_\\sigma)v=0\\) for some vector \\(v\\in\\mathbb R^n\\).  Then\n\\[\nJv = P_\\sigma v. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe left\u2011hand side equals \\((\\sum_{i=1}^n v_i)\\mathbf 1\\), where \\(\\mathbf 1=(1,1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\).  The right\u2011hand side is a permutation of the coordinates of \\(v\\).  Hence all coordinates of \\(v\\) must be equal; write \\(v_i=c\\) for all \\(i\\).  Substituting into (6) gives\n\\[\nnc\\,\\mathbf 1 = c\\,\\mathbf 1,\n\\]\nso \\((n-1)c=0\\).  Since \\(n>1\\), we obtain \\(c=0\\) and consequently \\(v=0\\).  Thus the kernel of \\(J-P_\\sigma\\) is trivial, implying that \\(J-P_\\sigma\\) is invertible and therefore has full rank \\(n\\). \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma to (1) yields\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-P_\\sigma)=n. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (7) we obtain the **lower bound**\n\\[\nk\\ge n. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nSelect any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) (for simplicity take \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\)).  For each row \\(i\\) place a single rectangular tile\n\\[\nT_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq \\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nGeometrically, \\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole row \\(i\\) except the square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- The tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) occupy distinct rows, so they are pairwise disjoint; no unit square is covered twice.  \n- In row \\(i\\) the only uncovered square is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) by construction.  \n- In column \\(j\\) the uncovered square is \\((\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)\\); all other squares of that column belong to the tile of their respective rows, so each column also contains exactly one uncovered square.  \n\nThus the tiling satisfies the problem\u2019s requirement and uses precisely \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nInequality (8) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles, while the construction of \u00a74 achieves exactly \\(n\\) tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n1. Every statement in the Detailed Solution follows logically from previous ones, with no hidden assumptions.\n2. The rank lemma is proved completely (kernel argument) and does not rely on unstated facts.\n3. The construction is explicitly checked for each row and column, confirming the \u201cexactly one uncovered square\u2019\u2019 condition.\n4. All mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters."
