ut meeting a hole; it is a **maximal empty rectangle**. The same argument applies to \\(S_i\\).\n\n*Disjointness:*  \nThe row sets of \\(R_i\\) and \\(S_i\\) are \\([1,i]\\) and \\([i+1,n]\\), respectively, which are disjoint. Consequently all \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i,S_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\nThus the board always contains at least \\(2(n-1)\\) distinct maximal empty rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nLet a tile be any axis‑parallel rectangle that contains no hole. Starting from such a tile, repeatedly enlarge it in any direction until it can no longer be enlarged; the process stops at a maximal empty rectangle. Therefore **every tile is contained in a maximal empty rectangle**.\n\nSince the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i,S_i\\}\\) are disjoint, a single tile cannot intersect two different ones (otherwise the tile would contain a hole on the common boundary). Hence each of these rectangles requires at least one distinct tile. Consequently any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e., the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor this permutation we have  \n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{1,2,\\dots ,i\\},\\qquad D_i=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nR_i=[1,i]\\times[i+1,n],\\qquad \nS_i=[i+1,n]\\times[1,i].\n\\]\n\nThe rectangles \\(R_i\\) and \\(S_i\\) are exactly the “staircase’’ regions above and below the diagonal. They are disjoint and together cover every cell that is not on the diagonal. Using each of them as a tile yields a valid tiling with precisely  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\nThus the lower bound is attainable.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nFor an \\(n\\times n\\) board the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction In