                "content": ".\n\n**### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Reformulation.**  \n   - Choose a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)).  \n   - The uncovered squares are exactly \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\); all other squares must be tiled by axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles).  \n   - A tile corresponds to a pair of subsets \\((R,C)\\) of rows and columns with \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\); it covers the squares \\(\\{(i,j):i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound – distinguished squares.**  \n   - For each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i_j\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j+1\\).  \n   - Define the “left‑distinguished’’ square \\(L_j=(i_j,j)\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define the “right‑distinguished’’ square \\(R_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\).  \n   - **Lemma 1.** No allowed rectangle can contain two different \\(L_j\\)’s.  \n   - **Lemma 2.** No allowed rectangle can contain two different \\(R_i\\)’s, and a rectangle cannot contain simultaneously an \\(L_j\\) and an \\(R_i\\).  \n   - Consequently each rectangle can contain at most one square from the set \\(L\\cup R\\), which has size \\(|L|+|R|=2n-2\\).  \n   - Hence any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   - Use the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n     \\]  \n   - For each row \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n     \\]  \n   - The \\(V_j\\)’s cover all squares above the diagonal, the \\(H_i\\)’s cover all squares below the diagonal, and none of them contains a diagonal square.  \n   - The two families are pairwise disjoint, giving exactly