st contain the whole interval \\([j,j']\\), in particular column \\(j+1\\).  \nBut column \\(j+1\\) is the uncovered column of row \\(i_j\\) (by definition of \\(i_j\\)), and row \\(i_j\\in R\\); hence the rectangle would contain the uncovered square \\((i_j ,j+1)\\), contradicting (1). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nNo admissible rectangle can contain two distinct squares \\(R_i\\) and \\(R_{i'}\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\), and a rectangle cannot contain simultaneously a square \\(L_j\\) and a square \\(R_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe first statement is symmetric to Lemma 1 after swapping the roles of rows and columns.  \n\nFor the second statement, suppose a rectangle \\((R,C)\\) contains \\(L_j=(i_j ,j)\\) and \\(R_i=(i ,\\sigma(i)+1)\\).  \nThe column interval \\(C\\) contains both \\(j\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\).  \n\n- If \\(j<\\sigma(i)+1\\), then \\(C\\) also contains column \\(j+1=\\sigma(i_j)\\); since \\(i_j\\in R\\), the rectangle would contain the uncovered square \\((i_j ,\\sigma(i_j))\\).  \n\n- If \\(j>\\sigma(i)+1\\), then \\(C\\) contains column \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and also column \\(\\sigma(i)=\\sigma(i)+1-1\\); because \\(i\\in R\\), the rectangle would contain the uncovered square \\((i ,\\sigma(i))\\).  \n\nBoth possibilities contradict (1). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma 1, each rectangle can contain **at most one** square of the set \\(L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\); therefore at least \\(|L|=n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all squares of \\(L\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that a rectangle cannot contain a square of \\(L\\) together with a square of \\(R=\\{R_i\\}\\); consequently the same argument applied to \\(R\\) yields another \\(n-1\\) necessary rectangles.  \nSince the two families are disjoint, any tiling must use at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\n*Vertical rectangles (above the diagonal).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\\(V_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\); it does not contain the diagonal square \\((j,j)\\), and it lies entirely above the diagonal.\n\n*Horizontal rectangles (below the diagonal).*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\\(H_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\); it avoids the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\) and lies entirely below the diagonal.\n\n*Verification.*  \n- **Coverage.**  \n  *If \\(i<j\\)*, the square \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(V_j\\) because \\(i\\le j-1\\).  \n  *If \\(i>j\\)*, the square \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(H_i\\) because \\(j\\le i-1\\).  \n  Thus every off‑diagonal square is covered exactly once.  \n\n- **Disjointness.**  \n  Each \\(V_j\\) uses only column \\(j\\); each \\(H_i\\) uses only row \\(i\\).  \n  The only possible intersection of a \\(V_j\\) with an \\(H_i\\) is the square \\((i,j)\\).  \n  If \\(i=j\\) this square is diagonal and belongs to neither family; if \\(i\\neq j\\) the square lies either above or below the diagonal, hence belongs to exactly one of the two families.  \n  Therefore the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\nThere are \\((n-1)\\) vertical rectangles and \\((n-1)\\) horizontal rectangles, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nInequality (4) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\