rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row there is exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it therefore consists of all unit squares whose row index lies in a non‑empty interval \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and whose column index lies in a non‑empty interval \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe tile may be placed only if it does not contain any uncovered square, i.e.  \n\\[\nR\\cap\\sigma^{-1}(C)=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll unit squares not in \\(U\\) must be covered by such tiles, and no two tiles may overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Distinguished squares  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\\[\ni_j:=\\sigma^{-1}(j+1),\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nthe unique row whose uncovered square lies in column \\(j+1\\).  \nDefine the *left‑distinguished* square  \n\\[\nL_j:=(i_j,j).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define the *right‑distinguished* square  \n\\[\nR_i:=(i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince exactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\), there are precisely \\(n-1\\) squares of each type; the two families are disjoint because if \\((i_j,j)=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) then \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j+1=\\sigma(i)+2\\), contradicting \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j+1\\).\n\nEvery distinguished square is not uncovered, hence it must belong to some tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lemmas on rectangles  \n\n**Lemma 1.** No admissible rectangle can contain two distinct squares \\(L_j\\) and \\(L_{j'}\\) with \\(j<j'\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\((R,C)\\) contains \\((i_j,j)\\) and \\((i_{j'},j')\\).  \nSince both columns \\(j\\) and \\(j'\\) belong to the column interval \\(C\\), all columns between them, in particular column \\(j+1\\), also belong to \\(C\\).  \nColumn \\(j+1\\) is the uncovered column of row \\(i_j\\) (by (3)), and row \\(i_j\\in R\\).  \nThus the rectangle would contain the uncovered square \\((i_j,j+1)\\), contradicting (2). ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.** No admissible rectangle can contain two distinct squares \\(R_i\\) and \\(R_{i'}\\), nor can it contain simultaneously a square of \\(L\\) and a square of \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.* The first statement follows from Lemma 1 after interchanging the roles of rows and columns (the column interval of a rectangle containing \\(R_i\\) and \\(R_{i'}\\) must contain the uncovered column of one of the involved rows).  \n\nFor the mixed case, assume a rectangle \\((R,C)\\) contains \\(L_j=(i_j,j)\\) and \\(R_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\).  \nSince both columns \\(j\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) belong to \\(C\\), the interval \\(C\\) contains either \\(j+1\\) (if \\(j<\\sigma(i)+1\\)) or \\(\\sigma(i)\\) (if \\(j>\\sigma(i)+1\\)).  \n\n- If \\(j<\\sigma(i)+1\\), then column \\(j+1\\) is the uncovered column of row \\(i_j\\); because \\(i_j\\in R\\), the rectangle would contain the uncovered square \\((i_j,j+1)\\).  \n\n- If \\(j>\\sigma(i)+1\\), then column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) is the uncovered column of row \\(i\\); because \\(i\\in R\\), the rectangle would contain the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \n\nBoth possibilities contradict (2). ∎\n\nFrom Lemmas 1 and 2 we deduce:\n\n> **Corollary.** Every admissible rectangle can contain **at most one** square from the set \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nAll squares of \\(L\\cup R\\) must be covered, and each tile can cover at most one of them.  \nHence any tiling requires at least  \n\\[\n|L|+|R|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- If a square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(i<j\\) (above the diagonal), then \\(j\\ge2\\) and \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\) because \\(i\\le j-1\\).  \n\n- If \\(i>j\\) (below the diagonal), then \\(i\\ge2\\) and \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\) because \\(j\\le i-1\\).  \n\nThus every off‑diagonal square belongs to exactly one of the rectangles listed in (7)–(8).  \n\n- No rectangle contains a diagonal square: each \\(V_j\\) uses only column \\(j\\) and rows \\(1,\\dots ,j-1\\); the diagonal square in column \\(j\\) is \\((j,j)\\), which is not among those rows. Similarly each \\(H_i\\) uses only row \\(i\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,i-1\\); the diagonal square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,i)\\), which is not among those columns.  \n\nConsequently the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells, satisfying the requirement that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square.\n\nThe construction uses \\((n-1)\\) vertical rectangles and \\((n-1)\\) horizontal rectangles, for a total of \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (6) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** The smallest number of rectangular tiles that can be placed so that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NO