ext{or}\\quad B\\subseteq\\{p(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently one of the following holds for the whole rectangle:\n\n* **Left‑rectangle:** \\(\\displaystyle \\max B<\\min_{i\\in A}p(i)\\).  \n* **Right‑rectangle:** \\(\\displaystyle \\min B>\\max_{i\\in A}p(i)\\).\n\nThus each feasible rectangle is either left or right (but never both).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  At least \\(n-1\\) left‑rectangles  \n\nOrder the rows by the column of their hole:\n\n\\[\np(i_1)<p(i_2)<\\dots <p(i_n).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\nc_k=(i_k,\\,p(i_{k-1})).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p(i_{k-1})<p(i_k)\\), the cell \\(c_k\\) lies strictly left of the hole in row \\(i_k\\); therefore it must belong to a left‑rectangle.  \nLet this rectangle be \\(R_k=A_k\\times B_k\\).  \nBecause \\(c_k\\in R_k\\),\n\n\\[\ni_k\\in A_k,\\qquad p(i_{k-1})\\in B_k.\n\\]\n\nBeing left, \\(R_k\\) satisfies \\(\\max B_k<\\min_{i\\in A_k}p(i)\\); in particular \\(\\max B_k<p(i_k)\\).  \nTogether with \\(p(i_{k-1})\\in B_k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np(i_{k-1})\\le \\max B_k\\le p(i_k)-1.\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(p(i_{k-1})\\) and \\(p(i_k)\\) are consecutive in the ordered list, so the only integer in the interval \\([p(i_{k-1}),\\,p(i_k)-1]\\) is \\(p(i_{k-1})\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\max B_k = p(i_{k-1}).\n\\]\n\nThus the rightmost column of \\(R_k\\) is exactly \\(p(i_{k-1})\\).  \nSince the values \\(p(i_1),p(i_2),\\dots ,p(i_{n-1})\\) are distinct, the rectangles \\(R_2,R_3,\\dots ,R_n\\) have distinct rightmost columns and are therefore distinct.  \nConsequently any tiling contains **at least \\(n-1\\) left‑rectangles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  At least \\(n-1\\) right‑rectangles  \n\nApply the same reasoning to the transpose of the board, i.e. to the permutation \\(p^{-1}\\).  \nExactly the same argument shows that any tiling must contain at least \\(n-1\\) right‑rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the total number of rectangles  \n\nA rectangle cannot be simultaneously left and right (otherwise it would contain a hole).  \nHence the total number of rectangles in any tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \nDefine the following \\(2n-2\\) rectangles:\n\n* For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) the horizontal rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering the cells to the right of the hole in row \\(i\\).\n\n* For each \\(j=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) the vertical rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  an \\((n-j+1)\\times1\\) tile covering the cells below the hole in column \\(j\\).\n\nThe sets \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint; each cell of the board that is not on the diagonal belongs to exactly one of them, while the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered.  \nThus we have a valid tiling using exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
