nteriors are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Every tile is either left or right  \n\nA tile is a set \\(R=A\\times B\\) where \\(A\\subseteq[n]\\) and \\(B\\subseteq[n]\\) are intervals of consecutive rows and columns.  \nBecause \\(R\\) contains no hole, for each row \\(i\\in A\\) the column interval \\(B\\) cannot contain the column \\(p(i)\\). Hence for each such \\(i\\) either  \n\n\\[\n\\max B < p(i) \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad \\min B > p(i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf (1) holds with \\(\\max B < p(i)\\) for some \\(i\\) and with \\(\\min B > p(j)\\) for another \\(j\\) in the same \\(A\\), then we would have  \n\n\\[\n\\max B < p(i) \\le p(j) < \\min B,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because \\(\\max B < \\min B\\) cannot hold.  Consequently the same alternative in (1) holds for **all** rows of \\(A\\).  \n\nThus each tile is of one of the two types:\n\n* **Left tile:** \\(\\displaystyle \\max B < \\min_{i\\in A}p(i)\\).\n* **Right tile:** \\(\\displaystyle \\min B > \\max_{i\\in A}p(i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  At least \\(n-1\\) left tiles are necessary  \n\nReorder the rows so that  \n\n\\[\np(i_{1})<p(i_{2})<\\dots <p(i_{n}).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\nc_k=(i_{k},\\,p(i_{k-1})).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) we have \\(p(i_{k-1})<p(i_{k})\\); therefore \\(c_k\\) lies **left** of the hole in row \\(i_{k}\\).  Consequently \\(c_k\\) belongs to a left tile, say \\(R_k=A_k\\times B_k\\).\n\nSince \\(R_k\\) is left, \\(\\max B_k < \\min_{i\\in A_k}p(i)\\).  As \\(i_{k}\\in A_k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\max B_k < p(i_{k}).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c_k\\in R_k\\), we also have \\(p(i_{k-1})\\in B_k\\), whence  \n\n\\[\np(i_{k-1})\\le \\max B_k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) and (4) we deduce  \n\n\\[\np(i_{k-1})\\le \\max B_k \\le p(i_{k})-1.\n\\]\n\nSince the integers \\(p(i_{k-1})\\) and \\(p(i_{k})\\) are consecutive in the ordered list (2), the only integer satisfying the double inequality is \\(p(i_{k-1})\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\max B_k = p(i_{k-1}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the rightmost column of the left tile containing \\(c_k\\) is exactly \\(p(i_{k-1})\\).  The numbers \\(p(i_{1}),p(i_{2}),\\dots ,p(i_{n-1})\\) are distinct, so the tiles \\(R_2,R_3,\\dots ,R_n\\) are all different.  Therefore any tiling uses at least \\(n-1\\) left tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  At least \\(n-1\\) right tiles are necessary  \n\nApply the same reasoning to the transpose of the board (or equivalently to the permutation \\(p^{-1}\\)).  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nd_k=(i_{k},\\,p(i_{k+1}))\n\\]\n\nlies **right** of the hole in row \\(i_{k}\\); it must belong to a right tile.  By an argument symmetric to the one above, the leftmost column of that right tile equals \\(p(i_{k+1})\\).  Since the columns \\(p(i_{2}),\\dots ,p(i_{n})\\) are distinct, we obtain at least \\(n-1\\) right tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the total number of tiles  \n\nA tile cannot be simultaneously left and right, because the two definitions are mutually exclusive.  Consequently any family \\(\\mathcal T\\) of tiles covering \\(C\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFix the permutation \\(p\\) that determines the holes.  For each row \\(i\\) define\n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\}, & \\text{if }p(i)>1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\varnothing, & \\text{if }p(i)=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nR_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}, & \\text{if }p(i)<n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\varnothing, & \\text{if }p(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nEach non‑empty \\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is a rectangle of height 1 and width \\(p(i)-1\\) (resp. \\(n-p(i)\\)).  Because the rows are distinct, all these rectangles are pairwise disjoint.  Moreover, every square of \\(C\\) belongs either to the left segment of its row or to the right segment of its row, so the union of all non‑empty \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) equals \\(C\\).\n\nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) (hence no left segment) and exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\) (hence no right segment).  All remaining \\(n-2\\) rows contribute both a left and a right segment.  Therefore the total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)\\times2+2 = 2n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (6) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction (7) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
