ax B < p(i)) or entirely right (min B > p(i)). The solution claims that for a given tile, the same alternative must hold for all rows i in A, else contradiction. Let's verify: Suppose there exist i,j in A such that for i, max B < p(i) and for j, min B > p(j). Then we have max B < p(i) and p(j) < min B. Since max B < min B, but p(i) and p(j) are integers, we need to check if possible. The solution says then we would have max B < p(i) ≤ p(j) < min B, which is impossible because max B < min B cannot hold. Actually max B < min B is always true? Wait, max B is the maximum column index in B, min B is the minimum column index in B. Since B is a set of consecutive columns, max B >= min B. Actually max B >= min B always. So max B < min B is impossible. So indeed can't have both left and right for different rows. So tile must be either left or right. Good.\n\nNow they argue at least n-1 left tiles. They reorder rows by increasing p(i). For each k=2..n, they consider cell (i_k, p(i_{k-1})). Since p(i_{k-1}) < p(i_k), this cell is left of the hole in row i_k. So it belongs to a left tile. They then argue that each such left tile is distinct for each k, thus at least n-1 left tiles. Let's verify distinctness: They show that the rightmost column of left tile containing c_k is exactly p(i_{k-1}) (by reasoning). So each left tile's rightmost column is p(i_{k-1}) for k=2..n. Since these are distinct values, the left tiles are distinct. However, could two left tiles have same rightmost column but different rows? The reasoning shows that each left tile's rightmost column is p(i_{k-1}) and also that i_k is in A_k (the rows of the tile). Could there be a left tile that contains multiple c_k for different k? Let's see: Suppose tile R_k contains c_k = (i_k, p(i_{k-1})). Could it also contain c_{k'} for some k' > k? That would require that p(i_{k-1}) and p(i_{k'-1}) are both in B_k, but B_k's max column = p(i_{k-1}) (they show max B_k = p(i_{k-1})). So cannot contain any column > p(i_{k-1}). Since p(i_{k'-1}) > p(i_{k-1}) for k'>k, cannot be in B_k. So each left tile contains exactly one c_k. So distinct. So at least n-1 left tiles.\n\nSimilarly for right tiles, they consider transpose and get at least n-1 right tiles.\n\nThus total at least 2n-2.\n\nConstruction: For each row i, left segment L_i = columns 1..p(i)-1 (if p(i)>1) and right segment R_i = columns p(i)+1..n (if p(i)<n). These are rectangles of height 1 (single row). They are disjoint because rows are distinct. So total tiles = number of non-empty L_i + number of non-empty R_i. Exactly one row has p(i)=1 (no left segment) and one row has p(i)=n (no right segment). So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. Yes.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity: Are the methods valid? They use combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, classification of tiles, counting arguments, construction. All are valid methods. They also rely on a claim about distinctness of left tiles based on column positions. That's a logical deduction using properties of permutations and intervals. No invalid reasoning.\n\nThus Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation issues. Let's see if there are any justification gaps or critical errors.\n\nPotential issues: The solution uses \"Since the integers p(i_{k-1}) and p(i_{k}) are consecutive in the ordered list (2), the only integer satisfying the double inequality is p(i_{k-1}).\" Actually they claim p(i_{k-1}) ≤ max B_k ≤ p(i_k)-1, and since p(i_{k-1}) and p(i_k) are consecutive values in the sorted list of p(i), there is no integer between them, so max B_k must equal p(i_{k-1}). That's correct.\n\nPotential minor gaps: They assume that there is exactly one row with p(i)=1 and one with p(i)=n. In a permutation of 1..n, indeed there is exactly one row where p(i)=1 and one where p(i)=n. So fine.\n\nPotential missing justification: They claim \"A tile cannot be simultaneously left and right, because the two definitions are mutually exclusive.\" That's obvious.\n\nPotential missing detail: In the construction, they claim \"Because the rows are distinct, all these rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\" That's correct: each L_i and R_i are in distinct rows, so they don't intersect. However, L_i and R_i for the same row are disjoint because they are separated by the uncovered column. So fine.\n\nPotential missing justification: They claim \"Exactly one row has p(i)=1 (hence no left segment) and exactly one row has p(i)=n (hence no right segment).\" That's true because p is a permutation, but they didn't explicitly state that. It's a small justification gap but acceptable.\n\nPotential missing detail: They didn't explicitly argue that the left/right tile classification covers all tiles. But they argued that each tile must be left or right. So fine.\n\nPotential missing detail: They didn't consider possibility of tiles that are empty (height zero) or width zero; but those are not tiles. So fine.\n\nPotential missing detail: They didn't discuss possibility of tiles that are not left or right because they might have B interval that does not intersect any p(i) for rows in A, but they argued that for each row i in A, B cannot contain p(i), so B must be either entirely left or entirely right of p(i). But if p(i) vary across rows, B could be left of some p(i) and right of others, but they argued that's impossible because then max B < p(i) and min B > p(j) for some i,j leads to contradiction. That's valid.\n\nPotential missing detail: The argument that \"max B < p(i) ≤ p(j) < min B\" uses ordering of p(i) and p(j). They assume p(i) ≤ p(j). But they didn't specify ordering; they just said if both left and right hold for different rows, then there exist i,j such that max B < p(i) and min B > p(j). Then they claim p(i) ≤ p(j) because they can reorder rows. Actually they need to ensure that p(i) ≤ p(j). But they can choose i and j such that p(i) ≤ p(j). Since max B < p(i) and min B > p(j) then max B < p(i) ≤ p(j) < min B, contradiction. So fine.\n\nThus overall reasoning is valid.\n\nNow presentation: Are there any critical errors? The solution seems fairly rigorous. There might be minor justification gaps: they didn't explicitly state that there is exactly one row with p(i)=1 and one with p(i)=n, but that is a small gap. Also they didn't explicitly prove that the left and right tiles are distinct for each k, but they gave reasoning. So no critical errors.\n\nThus Level 3: only justification gaps (if any). According to policy, justification gaps are acceptable, leading to PASS.\n\nThus final verdict: PASS.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON with fields: verdict, confidence, issues, answer_correctness, reasoning.\n\nConfidence: high, maybe 0.99.\n\nIssues: list any presentation issues found. Could list \"Justification gap: did not explicitly state that exactly one row has p(i)=1 and one row has p(i)=n, but this follows from permutation property.\" Also maybe \"Justification gap: did not explicitly mention that rows are distinct ensures rectangles are disjoint.\" But these are minor.\n\nThus issues list can include objects with type and description. The schema for issues not defined explicitly, but we can include a list of strings or objects. The instruction says \"issues\": [].\n\nWe can include a list of strings describing justification gaps.\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"PASS\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.99,\n  \"issues\": [\n    \"Justification gap: The solution does not explicitly state that exactly one row has p(i)=1 and one row has p(i)=n, though this follows from the permutation property.\",\n    \"Justification gap: The disjointness of the constructed rectangles L_i and R_i is asserted without explicit justification that rows are distinct and the intervals do not overlap.\"\n  ],\n  \"answer_correctness\": \"CORRECT\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"The solution correctly identifies the uncovered squares as a permutation matrix, proves a lower bound of 2n-2 tiles using left/right tile classifica