            "content": "**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Observation:** The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: exactly one uncovered cell in each row and each column, i.e. a set \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \n2. **Decomposition of the board:** For each row \\(i\\) define the *left part* \\(L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\}\\) and the *right part* \\(R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}\\).  The whole board minus the uncovered squares is the disjoint union  \n   \\[\n   L=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n   \\]  \n   Any tile (axis‑parallel rectangle) lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\); it cannot contain cells on both sides of the same hole.  \n3. **Lower bound – left side:**  \n   - Order the rows by the column of their hole: let \\(r_k\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(r_k)=k\\) (\\(k=1,\\dots ,n\\)).  \n   - For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) consider the *critical cell* \\(c_k=(r_k,k-1)\\in L\\) (the rightmost cell of the left part of row \\(r_k\\)).  \n   - **Lemma 1:** No rectangle can contain two distinct critical cells \\(c_k\\) and \\(c_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).  \n     *Proof:* If a rectangle contained both, its column interval would contain both \\(k-1\\) and \\(\\ell-1\\), so its rightmost column \\(\\ge\\ell-1\\). The rectangle also contains row \\(r_k\\), whose hole is at column \\(k\\); therefore the rectangle’s column interval must lie entirely left of column \\(k\\), a contradiction.  \n   - Since the \\(n-1\\) critical cells are pairwise exclusive, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\).  \n4. **Lower bound – right side:**  \n   - Symmetrically, for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) consider the critical cell \\(d_k=(r_k,k+1)\\in R\\) (the leftmost cell of the right part of row \\(r_k\\)).  \n   - **Lemma 2:** No rectangle can contain two distinct cells \\(d_k\\) and \\(d_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\). The proof is identical to Lemma 1, using the fact that a rectangle covering both would have to extend right of column \\(k+1\\) while staying left of the hole in row \\(r_k\\).  \n   - Hence at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(R\\).  \n5. **Combined lower bound:** Any tiling of the whole board must use at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  \n6. **Construction attaining the bound:**  \n   - Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) place a *left tile* covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\); this is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle.  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) place a *right tile* covering the cells \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\); this is a \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle.  \n   - The two tiles in a given row are disjoint (different column intervals), and tiles belonging to different rows are disjoint (different rows).  \n   - Every cell not on the diagonal belongs to exactly one of these tiles, and each row and each column has precisely the diagonal cell uncovered.  \n   - The construction uses exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.  \n7. **Conclusion:** The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the set  \n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\nof unit squares.  \nA *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently a tile is a set  \n\\[\nT=I\\times J,\\qquad I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of consecutive integers.  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a bijection\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nNo tile may contain a point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right parts\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nClearly \\(B\\setminus U=L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).  \nIf a tile \\(T=I\\times J\\) contains a cell of \\(L\\) then every column of \\(J\\) is \\(<\\sigma(i)\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\); hence \\(J\\) lies entirely to the left of the holes of the rows in \\(I\\).  Consequently a tile cannot contain cells from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\).  Thus the family of tiles splits into two subfamilies, one covering \\(L\\) and the other covering \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for covering \\(L\\)\n\nLet \\(r_k\\) denote the unique row with \\(\\sigma(r_k)=k\\) (so \\(\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \nFor each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) consider the *critical cell*\n\\[\nc_k=(r_k,k-1)\\in L_k\\subseteq L .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nNo rectangle \\(I\\times J\\) can contain two distinct critical cells \\(c_k\\) and \\(c_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(c_k\\) and \\(c_\\ell\\) belong to the same rectangle.  \nThen \\(k-1,\\ell-1\\in J\\); hence \\(\\max J\\ge\\ell-1\\).  \nSince the rectangle also contains row \\(r_k\\), its column interval must lie completely left of the hole of row \\(r_k\\), i.e. \\(\\max J<\\sigma(r_k)=k\\).  \nThus \\(\\max J<k\\le\\ell-1\\), contradicting \\(\\max J\\ge\\ell-1\\). ∎\n\nThe \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{c_2,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\) are pairwise exclusive for rectangles, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all of \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for covering \\(R\\)\n\nSymmetrically, for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define the critical cell\n\\[\nd_k=(r_k,k+1)\\in R_k\\subseteq R .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nNo rectangle can contain two distinct cells \\(d_k\\) and \\(d_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a rectangle contained both, its column interval would contain \\(k+1\\) and \\(\\ell+1\\), so \\(\\min J\\le k+1\\).  \nBecause the rectangle also contains row \\(r_k\\), all its columns must lie to the right of the hole of row \\(r_k\\), i.e. \\(\\min J>\\sigma(r_k)=k\\).  \nThus \\(k<\\min J\\le k+1\\), forcing \\(\\min J=k+1\\).  \nBut then the rectangle also contains column \\(\\ell+1\\ge k+2\\), contradicting \\(\\min J=k+1\\). ∎\n\nHence at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Combined lower bound\n\nAny tiling of the whole board consists of a family covering \\(L\\) and a family covering \\(R\\); the two families are disjoint.  \nFrom Sections 3 and 4 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) we place:\n\n* a *left tile*  \n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}= \\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,i-1\\},\n  \\qquad\\text{if }i\\ge2;\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n* a *right tile*  \n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}= \\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n  \\qquad\\text{if }i\\le n-1.\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\nEach \\(T_i^{\\text{L}}\\) (resp. \\(T_i^{\\text{R}}\\)) is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) (resp. \\(1\\times(n-i)\\)) rectangle, hence a legal tile.  \n\n*Disjointness.*  \nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the two tiles occupy disjoint column intervals \\([1,i-1]\\) and \\([i+1,n]\\).  \nTiles belonging to different rows have different row indices, therefore they are disjoint as well.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\) lies either in the left interval \\(1\\le j\\le i-1\\) (hence in \\(T_i^{\\text{L}}\\)) or in the right interval \\(i+1\\le j\\le n\\) (hence in \\(T_i^{\\text{R}}\\)).  Thus all cells of \\(B\\setminus U\\) are covered exactly once.\n\n*Uncovered cells.*  \nBy construction the only uncovered cell in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,i)\\); similarly each column \\(j\\) contains the uncovered cell \\((j,j)\\).  Hence the required condition is satisfied.\n\nThe total number of tiles is\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (5) and the explicit construction (8) coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
