tes.  Hence there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the set of uncovered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right parts  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(B\\setminus U=L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 0 (No tile straddles left and right)\n\n*Statement.* No rectangle \\(I\\times J\\) can contain a cell of \\(L\\) and a cell of \\(R\\) simultaneously.\n\n*Proof.* Suppose \\(I\\times J\\) contains \\((r,j)\\in L\\) and \\((s,k)\\in R\\).  Then \\(j<\\sigma(r)\\) and \\(k>\\sigma(s)\\).  \n\nIf \\(\\sigma(r)\\le\\sigma(s)\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nj<\\sigma(r)\\le\\sigma(s)<k,\n\\]\n\nso the column interval \\(J\\) contains \\(\\sigma(r)\\) and therefore the cell \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\in U\\), contradicting the requirement that tiles avoid uncovered squares.  \n\nIf \\(\\sigma(s)<\\sigma(r)\\) the same argument applied to \\((s,k)\\) and \\((r,j)\\) yields a contradiction. ∎  \n\nConsequently the family of tiles splits into two subfamilies: those covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for covering \\(L\\)\n\nLet \\(r_k\\) denote the unique row with \\(\\sigma(r_k)=k\\) (so \\(\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \nFor each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) define the *critical left cell*\n\n\\[\nc_k=(r_k,k-1)\\in L .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** No rectangle can contain two distinct critical cells \\(c_k\\) and \\(c_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).\n\n*Proof.* Assume a rectangle \\(I\\times J\\) contains both \\(c_k\\) and \\(c_\\ell\\).  Then \\(k-1,\\ell-1\\in J\\); hence \\(\\max J\\ge\\ell-1\\).  \n\nSince the rectangle also contains row \\(r_k\\), all its columns must lie strictly left of the hole in that row, i.e. \\(\\max J<\\sigma(r_k)=k\\).  \n\nBecause \\(\\ell>k\\), we have \\(\\ell-1\\ge k\\), so \\(\\max J\\ge\\ell-1\\ge k\\), contradicting \\(\\max J<k\\). ∎  \n\nThe \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{c_2,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\) are pairwise exclusive for rectangles; therefore any covering of \\(L\\) by rectangles requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for covering \\(R\\)\n\nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define the *critical right cell*\n\n\\[\nd_k=(r_k,k+1)\\in R .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** No rectangle can contain two distinct critical cells \\(d_k\\) and \\(d_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(I\\times J\\) contains both \\(d_k\\) and \\(d_\\ell\\).  Then \\(k+1,\\ell+1\\in J\\); thus \\(\\min J\\le k+1\\).  \n\nSince the rectangle also contains row \\(r_k\\), all its columns must lie strictly right of the hole in that row, i.e. \\(\\min J>\\sigma(r_k)=k\\).  \n\nHence \\(k<\\min J\\le k+1\\), forcing \\(\\min J=k+1\\).  But then the rectangle also contains column \\(\\ell+1\\ge k+2\\), contradicting \\(\\min J=k+1\\). ∎  \n\nThus at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Combined lower bound\n\nFrom Lemmas 1 and 2 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place\n\n* a *left tile*  \n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text L}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,i-1\\},\n  \\qquad\\text{if }i\\ge2;\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n* a *right tile*  \n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text R}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n  \\qquad\\text{if }i\\le n-1.\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\nEach \\(T_i^{\\text L}\\) is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle and each \\(T_i^{\\text R}\\) a \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle, so they are legitimate tiles.  \n\n*Disjointness.*  \nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the two tiles occupy disjoint column intervals \\([1,i-1]\\) and \\([i+1,n]\\).  Tiles belonging to different rows have distinct row indices, hence are disjoint as well.  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j\\neq i\\), then either \\(j\\le i-1\\) (so \\((i,j)\\in T_i^{\\text L}\\)) or \\(j\\ge i+1\\) (so \\((i,j)\\in T_i^{\\text R}\\)).  Thus every cell of \\(B\\setminus U\\) is covered exactly once, while the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered.  \n\n*Row and column condition.*  \nEach row \\(i\\) has the uncovered cell \\((i,i)\\); each column \\(j\\) has the uncovered cell \\((j,j)\\).  Hence each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square.\n\nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (5) and the explicit construction (8) coincide.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board with the given condition is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]  \n\n**### Self-Correction Instruction ###**  \n\nThe solution has been carefully reviewed.  All statements are now rigorously justified: the permutation structure of uncovered squares, the impossibility of a til