f a suitable permutation.*  \n   Write each row index as \\(i=45k+r\\) with \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,44\\}\\) and \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,45\\}\\) and define  \n   \\[\n   p(i)=45(k+1)- (r-1)=45k+46-r .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Inside each block of 45 consecutive rows the values of \\(p\\) strictly decrease, while the first element of each block is larger than every element of the previous block.  Hence the longest decreasing subsequence has length \\(45\\) (any whole block) and the longest increasing subsequence also has length \\(45\\) (take the last element of each block).  Thus\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{LIS}(p)=\\operatorname{LDS}(p)=45 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Greedy decomposition into rectangles.*  \n   - **Left part.** While there remains an uncovered square left of its hole, let \\(c\\) be the smallest column containing such a square.  Let  \n     \\[\n     I_c=\\{\\,i\\mid p(i)>c\\,\\}\n     \\]\n     and place the rectangle \\(R_c=I_c\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,c\\}\\).  Because every row in \\(I_c\\) has its hole to the right of column \\(c\\), \\(R_c\\) contains no hole.  Removing \\(R_c\\) eliminates column \\(c\\) from the left side of all rows in \\(I_c\\).  Repeating this step produces exactly \\(\\operatorname{LDS}(p)=45\\) left‑aligned rectangles and covers **all** squares with column index smaller than the hole in their row.\n\n   - **Right part.** After the left side is exhausted, perform the symmetric procedure from the right: while an uncovered square lies to the right of its hole, let \\(c\\) be the largest column containing such a square, let  \n     \\[\n     J_c=\\{\\,i\\mid p(i)<c\\,\\}\n     \\]\n     and place the rectangle \\(S_c=J_c\\times\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Each \\(S_c\\) is right‑aligned and avoids holes; the process ends after exactly \\(\\operatorname{LIS}(p)=45\\) steps, covering all remaining squares.\n\n   The two families \\(\\{R_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{S_c\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (they occupy disjoint column intervals) and together cover every unit square except the \\(2025\\) uncovered ones.  Hence we obtain a tiling with exactly \\(45+45=90\\) rectangular tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(90\\) tiles, and the explicit construction above provides a tiling with exactly \\(90\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\boxed{90}."
