                    "reasoning_content": "We need to verify the solution provided for the problem. The problem: 2025x2025 grid of unit squares, place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned with grid lines) covering some squares, each unit square at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles needed so that each row and each column has exactly one unit square not covered by any tile.\n\nSolution claims answer is 90. The solution uses a permutation model: uncovered squares form a permutation matrix. Then uses lower bound based on LIS+LDS >= 2 sqrt(n) = 90. Then constructs a permutation with LIS = LDS = 45, and a tiling with 45 left-aligned rectangles and 45 right-aligned rectangles covering all squares except holes, achieving 90 tiles. So answer 90.\n\nWe need to evaluate correctness of answer, reasoning validity, and presentation.\n\nFirst, check answer correctness. Is 90 indeed the minimum? Let's think about the problem. We need to cover all squares except exactly one per row and column (so uncovered squares form a permutation). Tiles are rectangles covering contiguous blocks of squares, no overlapping. We want to minimize number of tiles. The solution's lower bound uses LIS+LDS >= 2 sqrt(n). For n = 2025 = 45^2, this gives >= 90. The construction yields exactly 90. So answer 90 seems plausible. We need to verify if the lower bound is valid and the construction indeed works. The solution uses Lemma 1: number of tiles >= LIS(p) + LDS(p). Need to check if Lemma 1 is valid. The reasoning: choose longest increasing subsequence I and longest decreasing subsequence D that are vertex-disjoint (possible by Dilworth's theorem). For each i in I, take cell left of its hole; for each j in D, take cell right of its hole. The set S has size LIS+LDS. A rectangle that avoids all holes cannot contain two cells of S (otherwise its column interval would contain a hole). Hence each tile covers at most one cell of S; all cells of S must be covered, so at least |S| tiles needed. This s