e D. Since I and D are vertex-disjoint (no row appears in both), S contains at most one cell per row. The rectangle containing two cells from S would have to have column interval that includes both columns (p(i)-1) and (p(j)+1). Since p(i)-1 < p(i) < p(j) < p(j)+1 maybe? Actually need to check: For increasing subsequence I: p(i) values are increasing as i increases (or not necessarily i increasing? Usually LIS is subsequence of indices i1 < i2 < ... with p(i1) < p(i2) < ...). For decreasing subsequence D: indices i1 < i2 < ... but p(i1) > p(i2) > ... . So the left-of-hole cells for I are at columns p(i)-1, which are strictly increasing as well (since p(i) increasing). The right-of-hole cells for D are at columns p(i)+1, which are strictly decreasing (since p(i) decreasing). So the columns of left cells are increasing, columns of right cells are decreasing. If a rectangle contains one left cell and one right cell, the column interval must include both a left column and a right column. Since left column is less than its hole column, and right column is greater than its hole column, the interval includes the hole column(s). But need to ensure that the rectangle cannot contain any hole. The rectangle must avoid all holes. So indeed any rectangle covering two cells of S would contain a hole, violating condition. So each rectangle can cover at most one cell of S, thus number of tiles >= |S| = LIS + LDS. That seems valid.\n\nThus lower bound holds.\n\nNow the construction: The permutation p defined as p(i) = 45k+46 - r for i = 45k + r (k from 0 to 44, r from 1 to 45). So p(i) = 45(k+1) - (r-1) = 45k + 46 - r. For each block of 45 rows, the p-values are decreasing from 45k+45 down to 45k+1? Let's compute: For r=1, p = 45k+45? Actually p(i) = 45k+46 - r. For r=1, p = 45k+45. For r=45, p = 45k+1. So within each block, p decreases from 45k+45 to 45k+1. The first element of each block (r=1) is 45k+45. The next block's first element is 45(k+1)+45 = 45k+90? Wait compute: For block k+1 (k from 0 to 44), first element is 45(k+1)+45 = 45k+90? Actually block index goes from 0 to 44. For block k, rows i = 45k+1 to 45k+45. For block k+1, rows i = 45(k+1)+1 = 45k+46 to 45(k+1)+45 = 45k+90. The first element of block k+1 (i=45(k+1)+1) has r=1, k' = k+1, so p = 45(k+1)+45 = 45k+90. So indeed the first element of each block is larger than any element of previous block (since previous block's max is 45k+45). So the permutation consists of decreasing blocks of length 45, each block's values are a decreasing sequence, and the blocks are increasing in terms of values.\n\nThus LIS = 45 (by taking last element of each block: p(45k+45) = 45k+1, which is increasing across blocks). LDS = 45 (by taking whole block). So LIS+LDS = 90.\n\nNow the greedy decomposition: They define left part: while there remains an uncovered square left of its hole, let c be smallest column containing such a square. Let I_c = {i | p(i) > c}. Then rectangle R_c = I_c x {1,...,c}. Since for each i in I_c, p(i) > c, the hole is to the right of column c, so rectangle does not contain any hole. So they can cover all squares left of holes in those rows. They claim this process yields exactly LDS = 45 left-aligned rectangles. Indeed, each step picks smallest column c that still has uncovered squares left of holes. For each step, the set I_c is the set of rows whose holes are to the right of c. As c increases, the set I_c shrinks. The number of steps equals number of distinct values of p(i) that are the minimal column where left side is exhausted? Actually they claim exactly LDS steps. Since LDS = 45, the number of distinct values of p(i) that can be used as thresholds? Let's examine: For each block of decreasing values, the smallest column that still has uncovered squares left of holes is the smallest column among all holes? Actually we need to see that each step reduces the set of rows by at least one? Let's think.\n\nGiven the permutation, we can think of the shape of holes: each row i 