gain is okay (overlap?), but we need tiles to be disjoint? Actually they need to be pairwise disjoint rectangles covering distinct squares. The algorithm says \"place rectangle R_c = I_c × {1,...,c}\". Since I_c includes rows that have holes to the right of column c, and we are covering columns 1..c for those rows. However, the rectangle for c=2 includes column 1 again for those rows, overlapping with R_1. But they claim rectangles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy disjoint column intervals. Wait R_c uses columns {1,...,c}. So R_1 uses columns {1}. R_2 uses columns {1,2}. That overlaps column 1. So they are not disjoint. However, perhaps they intend to remove rows that have been covered already? Actually after placing R_1, the rows in I_1 are now fully covered for column 1. But the algorithm may then consider rows that still have uncovered squares left of holes after removing those covered squares. But they didn't remove rows from I_c after each step. Let's read the description: \"While there remains an uncovered square left of its hole, let c be the smallest column containing such a square. Let I_c = {i | p(i) > c} and place rectangle R_c = I_c × {1,2,...,c}. Because every row in I_c has its hole to the right of column c, R_c contains no hole. Removing R_c eliminates column c from the left side of all rows in I_c. Repeating this step produces exactly LDS(p) left‑aligned rectangles\". So after placing R_c, they \"remove column c from the left side of all rows in I_c\". That means they consider the uncovered squares left of holes after removing the rectangle. So after R_1, column 1 is covered for rows in I_1. Then for c=2, we need to consider rows that still have uncovered squares left of holes. For those rows, column 2 is the smallest column with uncovered left squares. But for rows that already had column 1 covered, they still have column 2 uncovered if p(i) > 2. So I_2 = rows with p(i) > 2. However, the rectangle R_2 = I_2 × {1,2} would cover column 1 again for those rows, which is already covered. But perhaps they consider that we are not allowed to overlap; but they might be using the fact that after R_1, rows in I_1 have column 1 covered, but they still have column 2 uncovered. So we could place rectangle covering columns 2 only (i.e., {2}) for those rows. But they defined rectangle as columns {1,...,c} to simplify. However, they might be implicitly assuming that after each step, we consider the remaining uncovered squares and the rectangle R_c covers only the new column c (since columns 1..c-1 are already covered for those rows). But the rectangle definition includes them, but they are already covered, so the rectangle would overlap with previous rectangles, which is not allowed. So perhaps they intend to use the fact that after each step, the rows in I_c are reduced to those not already covered? Actually they define I_c = {i | p(i) > c}. This set includes rows that may have been partially covered in previous steps, but the rectangle R_c includes all columns up to c, which includes columns previously covered for those rows. So there would be overlap. But they might be assuming that we are placing these rectangles sequentially and covering squares that are not yet covered; the rectangle R_c may be placed over squares that are already covered, but that would be redundant and not allowed because each unit square can be covered at most once. So the algorithm as described seems flawed: the rectangles are not disjoint.\n\nBut perhaps they intend to define R_c as covering only column c for rows in I_c, but they wrote {1,...,c} for simplicity, but the actual covering is only the new column. However, the description says \"R_c = I_c × {1,2,...,c}\". That is a rectangle covering columns 1..c. If we place that rectangle after previous rectangles, there will be overlap. So the algorithm as described is not correct as written. However, perhaps they intend to place rectangles in order of decreasing c (largest column first) to 