cal errors. The overlapping rectangle claim is a critical error. So we would mark as FAIL.\n\nBut we need to be careful: Is the claim \"pairwise disjoint\" indeed false? Let's examine the left part: they claim \"The two families {R_c} and {S_c} are pairwise disjoint (they occupy disjoint column intervals)\". Indeed, left part rectangles occupy columns up to c, right part rectangles occupy columns from c to n. The left part rectangles occupy columns from 1 to c for each c. So they occupy overlapping column intervals among themselves, but they are disjoint from the right part rectangles because left part columns are ≤ some c, right part columns are ≥ some c. However, left part rectangles themselves are not disjoint. The phrase \"pairwise disjoint\" could refer to the two families collectively (i.e., any rectangle from left family is disjoint from any rectangle from right family). But they said \"pairwise disjoint (they occupy disjoint column intervals)\". That suggests each rectangle occupies a distinct column interval, which is false. So it's a misstatement. However, maybe they meant that each rectangle in left family occupies a distinct column interval from each rectangle in right family, not within the same family. But they said \"pairwise disjoint\" which usually means all rectangles are disjoint from each other. So it's ambiguous. The solution might be considered to have a justification gap (lack of clarity) rather than a critical error. But the overlapping rectangles would violate the condition that each unit square is covered at most once. If the solution's construction indeed overlaps, then the construction fails to produce a valid tiling. That is a critical error because the claimed construction does not satisfy the problem constraints. So we must decide if the solution's construction is indeed invalid as described. The description is ambiguous, but the claim that the rectangles cover all squares left of holes and are pairwise disjoint is false if we interpre