re \\((s_c,c)\\).  \nHence the **left side** of that tile coincides with the cut \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nThus for every \\(c\\) there exists a tile whose left side is \\(\\ell_c\\).\n\nA rectangle cannot have both its left side and its right side on the same cut \\(\\ell_c\\), because that would require its leftmost column to be \\(c+1\\) and its rightmost column to be \\(c\\), which is impossible.  \nTherefore the tiles guaranteed for the right side of \\(\\ell_c\\) and those guaranteed for the left side of \\(\\ell_c\\) are distinct.\n\nSince there are \\(n-1\\) cuts and each cut contributes one distinct tile for its right side and one distinct tile for its left side, we obtain\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|\\;\\ge\\;2(n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_k&=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\\\\[2pt]\nH_k&=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(V_k\\) is a vertical rectangle of width \\(k\\) and height \\(n-k\\);  \n\\(H_k\\) is a horizontal rectangle of width \\(n-k\\) and height \\(k\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nIf a point \\((i,j)\\) belongs to some \\(V_k\\) then \\(j\\le k\\) and \\(i\\ge k+1\\).  \nIf it belongs to some \\(H_{k'}\\) then \\(i\\le k'\\) and \\(j\\ge k'+1\\).  \nBoth inequalities cannot hold simultaneously, so the families \\(\\{V_k\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((i,j)\\) be a unit square not on the diagonal, i.e. \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n- If \\(i>j\\) set \\(k=j\\). Then \\(j\\le k\\) and \\(i\\ge k+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in V_k\\).\n- If \\(i<j\\) set \\(k=i\\). Then \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(j\\ge k+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in H_k\\).\n\nThus every off‑diagonal square belongs to exactly one of the rectangles defined in (3).  \nThe diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) belong to none of them, so they remain uncovered.\n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T'=\\{V_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{H_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nis a valid tiling consisting of \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(|\\mathcal T|\\ge 2(n-1)\\) for any admissible tiling.  \nThe construction above shows that \\(|\\mathcal T|=2(n-1)\\) is achievable.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
